{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"Vibe testing is basically a quick, informal way of checking whether an LLM\\u2011driven application seems to work. Imagine you sit down, fire off five or ten prompts that come to mind, read the answers, and then decide \\u201cit feels right\\u201d if the responses look good. The whole process is driven by that gut feeling\\u2014you\\u2019re judging the output by how it *feels* rather than by any systematic criteria.\\n\\nBecause it relies on a handful of ad\\u2011hoc questions, vibe testing is highly subjective. What feels correct today might not feel the same a week later, especially since you probably won\\u2019t repeat exactly the same set of prompts. In other words, it isn\\u2019t repeatable: you can\\u2019t reliably reproduce the test or compare results across runs. That makes it suitable only for personal tinkering or a quick sanity check, not for the rigor that production software demands.\\n\\nWhen you try to launch an LLM application into a real product, you need more than a pleasant \\u201cvibe.\\u201d You need methodological evaluation\\u2014structured test suites, coverage of edge cases, quantitative metrics, and repeatable procedures that give you confidence the model will behave correctly under a wide range of inputs. Relying solely on vibe testing means you might miss systematic errors, biases, or failure modes that only show up outside your small, hand\\u2011picked prompt set. As a result, teams that have deployed an LLM app after only vibe testing have often run into problems because the informal check never proved the system robust enough for production use.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is vibe testing and why isn't it enough for deploying an llm app?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"applications. But if I ask the next question, have you ever evaluated it properly? Have you ever made an application or a chatbot? Have you ever evaluated it methodologically? So what would you say? I feel a lot of you will say that sir, I haven't evaluated it properly. But I asked him questions and I found the answer to it. So I assumed that this application is working properly. Right? Mostly we do this. What we do is we build an LLME-based application. Then before checking it, before launching it, before sharing it with someone, we ask the 4-5 questions that come to our mind at that point. And that's it. If the right responses come there, then we accept that our application is working properly. Actually, this whole phenomenon, this way of testing, this term has been given in the industry. It is called vibe testing. Just like vibe coding, it is vibe testing. What is vibe testing? Look, I have written it here. Vibe testing means you casually trying an LLME application with a few\", \"coding, it is vibe testing. What is vibe testing? Look, I have written it here. Vibe testing means you casually trying an LLME application with a few prompts and judging it by feel. You asked 5 questions and read the answers. And judge based on the feel whether it is right or not. This is called vibe testing. So basically what you do is you ask 5-10 questions, the answer is right, you feel that it is working. It is informal, it is subjective, and it is usually not repeatable. Because today you asked 5 questions, after 10 days you won't ask the same 5 questions, you will ask something else. So it is not even repeatable. Now you must be understanding even after listening to vibe testing that it works only at the personal project level. By doing vibe testing, you don't make production grade software in the industry. Obviously. Right? Now I will tell you some instances, some case studies where teams made a mistake that they deployed a LLME based application by doing vibe testing. And I\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response fully addressed the question about vibe testing and its insufficiency for deploying an LLM app, with no irrelevant content.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Vibe testing is basically a quick, informal way of checking whether an LLM\u2011driven application seems to work.\",\n    \"Imagine you sit down, fire off five or ten prompts that come to mind, read the answers, and then decide \u201cit feels right\u201d if the responses look good.\",\n    \"The whole process is driven by that gut feeling\u2014you\u2019re judging the output by how it feels rather than by any systematic criteria.\",\n    \"Because it relies on a handful of ad\u2011hoc questions, vibe testing is highly subjective.\",\n    \"What feels correct today might not feel the same a week later, especially since you probably won\u2019t repeat exactly the same set of prompts.\",\n    \"In other words, it isn\u2019t repeatable: you can\u2019t reliably reproduce the test or compare results across runs.\",\n    \"That makes it suitable only for personal tinkering or a quick sanity check, not for the rigor that production software demands.\",\n    \"When you try to launch an LLM application into a real product, you need more than a pleasant \u201cvibe.\u201d\",\n    \"You need methodological evaluation\u2014structured test suites, coverage of edge cases, quantitative metrics, and repeatable procedures that give you confidence the model will behave correctly under a wide range of inputs.\",\n    \"Relying solely on vibe testing means you might miss systematic errors, biases, or failure modes that only show up outside your small, hand\u2011picked prompt set.\",\n    \"As a result, teams that have deployed an LLM app after only vibe testing have often run into problems because the informal check never proved the system robust enough for production use.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The MMLU benchmark is a test suite that researchers use to see how well a language model knows factual information across a wide range of topics.\\u202fIn its original form it covered a very large \\u201cbreadth\\u201d of subjects\\u2014about 57 different areas\\u2014so that a model\\u2019s knowledge could be probed in many domains.\\u202fEach question in the original MMLU came with multiple\\u2011choice options, and the goal was simply to pick the correct one.\\n\\nBecause models eventually began to hit the ceiling on that version, the community created a follow\\u2011up called **MMLU\\u202fPro**.\\u202fIn the Pro version the number of subjects was trimmed down to roughly twelve, but the total number of questions stayed large (about 12\\u202f000, i.e., roughly 1\\u202f000 per subject).\\u202fEach question still offered ten answer choices, and the new set added more reasoning\\u2011heavy items, making the test a bit tougher.\\n\\nSo, in short, MMLU is a multi\\u2011subject, multiple\\u2011choice benchmark that evaluates a model\\u2019s factual and reasoning ability across many knowledge areas, and it served as a primary yardstick for LLM performance before newer benchmarks like GPQA, AGI\\u2011Eval, Simple\\u202fQA, and Humanity\\u2019s\\u202fLast\\u202fExam took over.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is the mmlu benchmark?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"but in MMLU Pro every question had 10 options so here you have to find the right answer but in 10 given options so you can see that the difficulty level increased after this what they did is they reduced the number of subjects from 57 they came down if I remember correctly they came down to 12 subjects and in 12 subjects they got 12k questions so per subject 1000 questions and they added some such questions which will give some reasoning so overall they made MMLU a little difficult so this for the next 1-2 years it was a good benchmark on which people kept testing their models basically MMLU Pro replaced MMLU but as it always happens that every next generation model beats the previous benchmark so over time what happened AGI eval also kind of saturated in GPQA also kind of near saturation and finally in 2025 a new benchmark came which is very popular and it is still used it's name is HLE Humanities Last Exam I can understand from the name how dangerous it is so in this people did that\", \"came then when MMLU was saturated people thought that we should test it with breadth of knowledge so then GPQA came and people thought that MMLU is a good benchmark we should repair it so in the process of repairing MMLU Pro came then slowly AGI eval, GPQA and MMLU Pro when all three of them started to saturate then finally people thought that we should make a new benchmark in which we will keep breadth and depth and some more innovations so we will keep it and call it humanity's last exam and this benchmark is still going on and when truthfull QA was saturated then we replaced it with simple QA so in total there are seven benchmarks that we are going to discuss today MMLU, truthfull QA AGI eval, GPQA MMLU Pro, simple QA humanity's last exam these are the seven benchmarks that we will discuss in detail but I have already told you an evolution story that why all these benchmarks came in picture and when they came in picture so let me know if this discussion made sense to you or if you\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, indicating perfect faithfulness to the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"In MMLU Pro every question had 10 options.\",\n    \"The difficulty level of MMLU Pro increased compared to the previous version.\",\n    \"The number of subjects was reduced from 57 to 12.\",\n    \"In the 12 subjects there are a total of 12,000 questions, which is about 1,000 questions per subject.\",\n    \"Some of the added questions require reasoning.\",\n    \"MMLU Pro replaced MMLU as a benchmark.\",\n    \"Over time, the AGI eval benchmark became saturated.\",\n    \"GPQA also approached saturation.\",\n    \"In 2025 a new benchmark called HLE (Humanities Last Exam) was introduced and is still used.\",\n    \"When MMLU was saturated, people decided to test breadth of knowledge, leading to the creation of GPQA.\",\n    \"During the process of repairing MMLU, MMLU Pro was created.\",\n    \"When AGI eval, GPQA, and MMLU Pro all started to saturate, a new benchmark called humanity's last exam was created.\",\n    \"When Truthful QA became saturated, it was replaced with Simple QA.\",\n    \"There are seven benchmarks discussed: MMLU, Truthful QA, AGI eval, GPQA, MMLU Pro, Simple QA, and humanity's last exam.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"The MMLU benchmark is a test suite that researchers use to see how well a language model knows factual information across a wide range of topics.\",\n    \"In its original form it covered a very large \u201cbreadth\u201d of subjects\u2014about 57 different areas\u2014so that a model\u2019s knowledge could be probed in many domains.\",\n    \"Each question in the original MMLU came with multiple\u2011choice options, and the goal was simply to pick the correct one.\",\n    \"Because models eventually began to hit the ceiling on that version, the community created a follow\u2011up called MMLU\u202fPro.\",\n    \"In the Pro version the number of subjects was trimmed down to roughly twelve, but the total number of questions stayed large (about 12\u202f000, i.e., roughly 1\u202f000 per subject).\",\n    \"Each question still offered ten answer choices, and the new set added more reasoning\u2011heavy items, making the test a bit tougher.\",\n    \"MMLU is a multi\u2011subject, multiple\u2011choice benchmark that evaluates a model\u2019s factual and reasoning ability across many knowledge areas.\",\n    \"MMLU served as a primary yardstick for LLM performance before newer benchmarks like GPQA, AGI\u2011Eval, Simple\u202fQA, and Humanity\u2019s\u202fLast\u202fExam took over.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context mentions MMLU as a benchmark but does not specify that it is used to assess factual knowledge across many topics, so the claim is not supported nor contradicted.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context provides details about MMLU Pro's options but does not describe the format of the original MMLU questions, so this claim cannot be verified.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context states that MMLU Pro was created during the repair of MMLU, but it does not attribute its creation to models hitting a performance ceiling.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"While the context confirms MMLU is a multi\\u2011subject benchmark, it does not explicitly state that it evaluates both factual and reasoning ability.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context lists newer benchmarks and notes that MMLU Pro replaced MMLU, but it does not say that MMLU was the primary yardstick before those newer benchmarks.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response fully answered the question about the MMLU benchmark with no irrelevant content.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"The MMLU benchmark is a test suite that researchers use to see how well a language model knows factual information across a wide range of topics.\",\n    \"In its original form it covered a very large \u201cbreadth\u201d of subjects\u2014about 57 different areas\u2014so that a model\u2019s knowledge could be probed in many domains.\",\n    \"Each question in the original MMLU came with multiple\u2011choice options, and the goal was simply to pick the correct one.\",\n    \"Models eventually began to hit the ceiling on that version.\",\n    \"The community created a follow\u2011up called MMLU\u202fPro.\",\n    \"In the Pro version the number of subjects was trimmed down to roughly twelve.\",\n    \"The total number of questions stayed large (about 12\u202f000, i.e., roughly 1\u202f000 per subject).\",\n    \"Each question still offered ten answer choices.\",\n    \"The new set added more reasoning\u2011heavy items, making the test a bit tougher.\",\n    \"MMLU is a multi\u2011subject, multiple\u2011choice benchmark that evaluates a model\u2019s factual and reasoning ability across many knowledge areas.\",\n    \"MMLU served as a primary yardstick for LLM performance before newer benchmarks like GPQA, AGI\u2011Eval, Simple\u202fQA, and Humanity\u2019s\u202fLast\u202fExam took over.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"MMLU\\u202fPro was built as a direct response to the shortcomings that people had pointed out in the original MMLU benchmark. The designers tackled two main problems.\\n\\nFirst, they made the multiple\\u2011choice questions harder. In the original MMLU each question offered four answer choices, so a test\\u2011taker could often get the right answer simply by eliminating three wrong ones. MMLU\\u202fPro expands the choice set to ten options per question. With ten alternatives you have to discard nine distractors, which raises the cognitive load and makes pure guesswork far less effective. In other words, the exam becomes noticeably more difficult because the elimination strategy that works with four options no longer suffices.\\n\\nSecond, they cleaned up the question pool itself. The earlier benchmark contained a lot of very basic items and \\u201cnoisy\\u201d questions that didn\\u2019t really probe deep understanding. MMLU\\u202fPro removes those easy or poorly\\u2011behaving items and replaces them with questions that require genuine reasoning. By focusing on reasoning\\u2011heavy problems, the benchmark better assesses whether a model can follow a chain of thought rather than just recall facts.\\n\\nTogether, these changes\\u2014more answer options and a curated set of reasoning\\u2011oriented questions\\u2014address the original MMLU\\u2019s criticism that it didn\\u2019t fully capture the core philosophy of testing knowledge and problem\\u2011solving ability. The result is a tougher, more diagnostic benchmark that pushes models to demonstrate more human\\u2011like reasoning rather than relying on superficial cues.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how did mmlu pro improve on the original mmlu?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"models are reaching humans in MMLU Pro there was no such human baseline in the paper they did not give it, whereas in the original MMLU, people criticized that the benchmark you have designed why are you not carrying the core philosophy that's it, it is not a big point shall we move to the next ok the next benchmark is interesting, in the sense that the benchmarks we discussed earlier are different in two aspects we will discuss both the aspects, before that I will tell you the story of this benchmark so if you remember, we discussed that everything started from MMLU one branch came out of it that take the exam like humans quickly tell us which benchmark was it, quick quiz in between just to check if you are retaining or not will anyone tell us which benchmark was it that said rather than creating new benchmarks why don't you use existing exams to check model intelligence or their knowledge which was it come on, no one remembers AGI well AGI well then who asked questions about\", \"so whatever problems were there in MM LU they tried to solve them let me tell you what things were solved the first change is that instead of giving 4 options in MCQ we give 10 options what will happen if we give 10 options according to you can you tell me if you are going to give one exam let's say IIT JEE or whatever other exams and instead of 4 options you get 10 options in every question so will your exam become more difficult or easy or I guess you can understand so we have given such exams and we try to eliminate options and reach the right option so when you have 4 options then elimination is easy you have to eliminate 3 and reach 1 when you have 10 then it will become very difficult so basically to increase the complexity researchers gave 10 options instead of 4 second, what they did is they removed the most basic questions and the noisy questions from MM LU and instead of them added reasoning based questions I will show you an example after a while but what did they do? MM LU\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, showing the actual output perfectly aligns with the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"The number of options in MCQs was changed from 4 to 10 in the described modification.\",\n    \"Providing 10 options instead of 4 makes elimination of incorrect answers more difficult.\",\n    \"Researchers increased complexity by giving 10 options instead of 4.\",\n    \"The most basic and noisy questions were removed from MMLU.\",\n    \"Reasoning-based questions were added to MMLU after removing the basic and noisy questions.\",\n    \"In MMLU Pro, models are reaching human performance.\",\n    \"The MMLU Pro paper did not include a human baseline.\",\n    \"In the original MMLU, there was criticism that the benchmark did not carry the core philosophy.\",\n    \"A subsequent benchmark differs from earlier benchmarks in two aspects.\",\n    \"A branch of MMLU was created that takes exams like humans.\",\n    \"Someone suggested using existing exams to assess model intelligence instead of creating new benchmarks.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"MMLU\u202fPro was built as a direct response to the shortcomings that people had pointed out in the original MMLU benchmark.\",\n    \"The designers tackled two main problems.\",\n    \"First, they made the multiple\u2011choice questions harder.\",\n    \"In the original MMLU each question offered four answer choices, so a test\u2011taker could often get the right answer simply by eliminating three wrong ones.\",\n    \"MMLU\u202fPro expands the choice set to ten options per question.\",\n    \"With ten alternatives you have to discard nine distractors, which raises the cognitive load and makes pure guesswork far less effective.\",\n    \"In other words, the exam becomes noticeably more difficult because the elimination strategy that works with four options no longer suffices.\",\n    \"Second, they cleaned up the question pool itself.\",\n    \"The earlier benchmark contained a lot of very basic items and \u201cnoisy\u201d questions that didn\u2019t really probe deep understanding.\",\n    \"MMLU\u202fPro removes those easy or poorly\u2011behaving items and replaces them with questions that require genuine reasoning.\",\n    \"By focusing on reasoning\u2011heavy problems, the benchmark better assesses whether a model can follow a chain of thought rather than just recall facts.\",\n    \"Together, these changes\u2014more answer options and a curated set of reasoning\u2011oriented questions\u2014address the original MMLU\u2019s criticism that it didn\u2019t fully capture the core philosophy of testing knowledge and problem\u2011solving ability.\",\n    \"The result is a tougher, more diagnostic benchmark that pushes models to demonstrate more human\u2011like reasoning rather than relying on superficial cues.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not state that MMLU\\u202fPro was built as a direct response to the shortcomings of the original MMLU benchmark.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context mentions a subsequent benchmark differing in two aspects, but it does not explicitly say that the designers tackled exactly two main problems.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context notes the addition of reasoning\\u2011heavy questions but does not mention assessment of a model's chain\\u2011of\\u2011thought capability.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"While the context describes increased difficulty and models reaching human performance, it does not explicitly claim that the benchmark pushes models to demonstrate more human\\u2011like reasoning rather than relying on superficial cues.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 0.8666666666666667, "reason": "The score is 0.87 because the response included non\u2011informative section headings ('First.' and 'Second.') that added no substance about how MMLU\u202fPro improves on the original MMLU, preventing a higher relevance rating.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"MMLU\u202fPro was built as a direct response to the shortcomings that people had pointed out in the original MMLU benchmark.\",\n    \"The designers tackled two main problems.\",\n    \"First.\",\n    \"They made the multiple\u2011choice questions harder.\",\n    \"In the original MMLU each question offered four answer choices, so a test\u2011taker could often get the right answer simply by eliminating three wrong ones.\",\n    \"MMLU\u202fPro expands the choice set to ten options per question.\",\n    \"With ten alternatives you have to discard nine distractors, which raises the cognitive load and makes pure guesswork far less effective.\",\n    \"The exam becomes noticeably more difficult because the elimination strategy that works with four options no longer suffices.\",\n    \"Second.\",\n    \"They cleaned up the question pool itself.\",\n    \"The earlier benchmark contained a lot of very basic items and \u201cnoisy\u201d questions that didn\u2019t really probe deep understanding.\",\n    \"MMLU\u202fPro removes those easy or poorly\u2011behaving items and replaces them with questions that require genuine reasoning.\",\n    \"By focusing on reasoning\u2011heavy problems, the benchmark better assesses whether a model can follow a chain of thought rather than just recall facts.\",\n    \"Together, these changes\u2014more answer options and a curated set of reasoning\u2011oriented questions\u2014address the original MMLU\u2019s criticism that it didn\u2019t fully capture the core philosophy of testing knowledge and problem\u2011solving ability.\",\n    \"The result is a tougher, more diagnostic benchmark that pushes models to demonstrate more human\u2011like reasoning rather than relying on superficial cues.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"It is merely a section heading ('First.') and does not provide any information about how MMLU\\u202fPro improves on the original MMLU.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"It is merely a section heading ('Second.') and does not convey any substantive detail about the improvements.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Sure, let me walk you through a concrete incident that illustrates how an LLM\\u2011driven application can go dramatically wrong in the real world.\\n\\nImagine you\\u2019re a traveler who has just lost a close family member and you need to fly home. In Canada, many airlines offer a \\u201cbereavement fare\\u201d\\u2014a special discount for people in exactly that situation. One customer went to Air Canada\\u2019s website to find out how that policy works. Instead of scrolling through a static FAQ, he opened the airline\\u2019s chatbot, which is powered by a large language model (LLM).\\n\\nAt this point the chatbot should have retrieved the correct, up\\u2011to\\u2011date policy from Air Canada\\u2019s internal knowledge base. But the model \\u201challucinated\\u201d\\u2014a technical term we use when an LLM generates information that sounds plausible yet is not grounded in reality. The bot told the user, \\u201cYou book a ticket now, pay the full amount, travel, and later we will refund your entire amount.\\u201d In other words, it fabricated a refund\\u2011after\\u2011travel policy that simply does not exist.\\n\\nRelying on that advice, the traveler booked a ticket, paid the full fare, and then, after traveling, asked for the promised refund. Customer\\u2011support, following the actual company policy, replied that Air Canada never offers post\\u2011travel refunds; the only discounts are applied before travel. The passenger was understandably upset and filed a legal case against the airline.\\n\\nDuring the court proceedings Air Canada\\u2019s lawyers tried to argue that the chatbot was a separate, autonomous entity and that the airline could disavow responsibility for what it said. The judge rejected that line of reasoning, stating that because the chatbot lives on Air Canada\\u2019s website\\u2014a property of the airline\\u2014the company is accountable for anything the bot communicates. Consequently, the court ordered Air Canada to pay roughly six hundred dollars in refunds and, more importantly, highlighted the reputational damage caused by the mistake.\\n\\nSo, this Air Canada episode is a textbook example of an LLM application failing badly: an AI\\u2011driven chatbot produced inaccurate, \\u201challucinated\\u201d information, leading a user to make a costly decision, sparking a customer\\u2011service dispute, and ultimately resulting in a legal judgment that held the airline liable for the bot\\u2019s false statements. It shows why rigorous testing, verification against authoritative sources, and clear responsibility frameworks are essential before deploying LLM\\u2011based tools in high\\u2011stakes settings.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"can you give a real-world example of an llm application failing badly?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"I will tell you some instances, some case studies where teams made a mistake that they deployed a LLME based application by doing vibe testing. And I will tell you the results with the help of 3 case studies. The first case study is Air Canada's chatbot. This was a very popular incident around 2-3 years ago. Where there was this guy and his grandmother died. And he was searching for bereavement fair on Air Canada's website. Bereavement fair is basically when someone in your family dies or your close friend dies and you have to travel, then airlines give you a discount because you are in a bad situation. I am not sure if this happens in India but it happens in Canada. So this guy went and on Air Canada's website he chatted with the chatbot about the bereavement fair policy. Now at this point the chatbot hallucinated and instead of telling the correct policy, he said that you book a ticket now, pay the full amount and travel. And later we will refund your entire amount. So this guy\", \"the correct policy, he said that you book a ticket now, pay the full amount and travel. And later we will refund your entire amount. So this guy booked the ticket and travelled. And after travelling when he tried to refund, customer support said that such a policy does not exist. We have this policy that you can take discounts before travelling. We do not refund anything later. So this guy got upset and filed a case against Air Canada. In the court, Air Canada's executives and lawyers pitched that the chatbot is a separate entity. Whatever he is saying, Air Canada does not know about it. It is like a separate entity. The judge said that this is not allowed. If your website is your property, then your chatbot is also your property. Which means that whatever your chatbot says, you will have to take its responsibility. Which means you will have to refund. So around 600 dollars of refund was to be done to Air Canada, which is not a big amount but it was a big blow on the name of a big\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, showing perfect alignment with the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"The Air Canada chatbot told a user to book a ticket, pay the full amount, travel, and that the entire amount would be refunded later.\",\n    \"The user booked the ticket and traveled based on the chatbot's advice.\",\n    \"When the user attempted to obtain a refund, customer support said that such a policy does not exist.\",\n    \"Air Canada's policy allows discounts before traveling but does not provide refunds later.\",\n    \"The user filed a case against Air Canada over the refund issue.\",\n    \"In court, Air Canada executives and lawyers claimed that the chatbot is a separate entity and that Air Canada does not know about its statements.\",\n    \"The judge ruled that because the website is Air Canada's property, the chatbot is also its property, making Air Canada responsible for the chatbot's statements.\",\n    \"The judge ordered Air Canada to refund approximately $600 to the user.\",\n    \"The incident involving Air Canada's chatbot occurred approximately 2-3 years ago.\",\n    \"The user was searching for information about a bereavement fare on Air Canada's website.\",\n    \"Bereavement fare is a discount offered by airlines when a traveler experiences a death in the family.\",\n    \"The chatbot hallucinated and provided incorrect policy information regarding refunds.\",\n    \"The text mentions case studies where teams deployed LLM-based applications without proper testing.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"In Canada, many airlines offer a \"bereavement fare\"\u2014a special discount for people in exactly that situation.\",\n    \"The airline\u2019s chatbot is powered by a large language model (LLM).\",\n    \"The bot told the user, \"You book a ticket now, pay the full amount, travel, and later we will refund your entire amount.\"\",\n    \"According to the narrative, Air Canada never offers post\u2011travel refunds; the only discounts are applied before travel.\",\n    \"The passenger filed a legal case against the airline.\",\n    \"Air Canada\u2019s lawyers argued that the chatbot was a separate, autonomous entity and that the airline could disavow responsibility for what it said.\",\n    \"The judge rejected that argument, stating that because the chatbot lives on Air Canada\u2019s website\u2014a property of the airline\u2014the company is accountable for anything the bot communicates.\",\n    \"The court ordered Air Canada to pay roughly six hundred dollars in refunds.\",\n    \"The court highlighted the reputational damage caused by the mistake.\",\n    \"The incident is presented as an example of an LLM application failing badly, producing inaccurate, hallucinated information, leading a user to make a costly decision, sparking a customer\u2011service dispute, and resulting in a legal judgment that held the airline liable for the bot\u2019s false statements.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context defines bereavement fare but does not state that many Canadian airlines offer it.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not mention the court highlighting reputational damage.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response directly addressed the request with a relevant real-world example and contained no irrelevant statements.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Sure, let me walk you through a concrete incident that illustrates how an LLM\u2011driven application can go dramatically wrong in the real world.\",\n    \"Imagine you\u2019re a traveler who has just lost a close family member and you need to fly home.\",\n    \"In Canada, many airlines offer a \u201cbereavement fare\u201d\u2014a special discount for people in exactly that situation.\",\n    \"One customer went to Air Canada\u2019s website to find out how that policy works.\",\n    \"Instead of scrolling through a static FAQ, he opened the airline\u2019s chatbot, which is powered by a large language model (LLM).\",\n    \"At this point the chatbot should have retrieved the correct, up\u2011to\u2011date policy from Air Canada\u2019s internal knowledge base.\",\n    \"But the model \u201challucinated\u201d\u2014a technical term we use when an LLM generates information that sounds plausible yet is not grounded in reality.\",\n    \"The bot told the user, \u201cYou book a ticket now, pay the full amount, travel, and later we will refund your entire amount.\u201d\",\n    \"In other words, it fabricated a refund\u2011after\u2011travel policy that simply does not exist.\",\n    \"Relying on that advice, the traveler booked a ticket, paid the full fare, and then, after traveling, asked for the promised refund.\",\n    \"Customer\u2011support, following the actual company policy, replied that Air Canada never offers post\u2011travel refunds; the only discounts are applied before travel.\",\n    \"The passenger was understandably upset and filed a legal case against the airline.\",\n    \"During the court proceedings Air Canada\u2019s lawyers tried to argue that the chatbot was a separate, autonomous entity and that the airline could disavow responsibility for what it said.\",\n    \"The judge rejected that line of reasoning, stating that because the chatbot lives on Air Canada\u2019s website\u2014a property of the airline\u2014the company is accountable for anything the bot communicates.\",\n    \"Consequently, the court ordered Air Canada to pay roughly six hundred dollars in refunds and, more importantly, highlighted the reputational damage caused by the mistake.\",\n    \"So, this Air Canada episode is a textbook example of an LLM application failing badly: an AI\u2011driven chatbot produced inaccurate, \u201challucinated\u201d information, leading a user to make a costly decision, sparking a customer\u2011service dispute, and ultimately resulting in a legal judgment that held the airline liable for the bot\u2019s false statements.\",\n    \"It shows why rigorous testing, verification against authoritative sources, and clear responsibility frameworks are essential before deploying LLM\u2011based tools in high\u2011stakes settings.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Sure, let\\u2019s unpack the idea of \\u201cdrift\\u201d and why it becomes a real concern once your model is out in the wild.\\n\\nThink of drift as any kind of change that makes the world your model sees look different from the world it was trained and evaluated on. In the material we discussed, drift isn\\u2019t limited to just one thing\\u2014it can show up in several guises. The most common flavors are **data drift**, where the actual input data start to shift; **model drift**, where the model\\u2019s behavior itself changes over time; and **concept drift**, which is a mismatch between what users expect and what the model delivers. There\\u2019s even a kind of \\u201cuser drift,\\u201d where the audience using the system changes \\u2013 for example, a platform that was originally popular with millennials might suddenly see a wave of Gen\\u2011Z users, and their expectations could be quite different.\\n\\nWhy does any of this matter after deployment? The key point is that the offline evaluations you performed before launch are based on a snapshot of data and user expectations at that moment. If the data, the model, the underlying concept, or the user base shifts later on, the performance you measured in the lab no longer guarantees the same experience for real users. In the example from the notes, a model that was scoring well offline could start receiving thumbs\\u2011downs because the new generation of users isn\\u2019t getting the answers they expect. That mismatch is precisely what drift captures.\\n\\nIn short, drift matters because it can silently erode the quality of your AI system after it\\u2019s live. Even if you keep the input dynamics static\\u2014say you have a fixed set of 100 documents you query against\\u2014other forms of drift (like changing user expectations or subtle model aging) can still appear. Recognizing that drift is inevitable in any AI\\u2011driven system helps you plan for ongoing monitoring and evaluation, rather than assuming that a one\\u2011time offline test will hold forever.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what is drift and why does it matter after deployment?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"seen this that at any point to perform the evaluation I am taking the last n conversations this is what you are saying I am trying to think what case can be where I simply take the last n conversations and my work will be done I am not able to figure out a scenario give me time I will research on my level one more question this is a bit basic generally we say that over time data drift but if I am a system where my input dynamics are not changing let's say I have sampled 100 documents and my work is just to query on it in this setup do you still think that after a while the data will drift and the data will not change or we have not given the user the freedom to pull the data drift has many natures you are talking about data drift you have controlled data drift because you haven't changed anything model can also drift in fact your user can also drift I am giving a very bad example let's say suddenly millennials used your platform and suddenly gen z's started using so from the\", \"user can also drift I am giving a very bad example let's say suddenly millennials used your platform and suddenly gen z's started using so from the expectation point of view there is a drift that what is he expecting, he is not getting the answer he is giving thumbs down now your current offline evaluation everything is working fine but your user who was a millennial before is not happy with it so that is also a drift which you have to see I don't know I haven't given a good example drift can be of many types we were talking about data drift but model drift and I am trying to think what other types of drift can be concept drift what I have discussed is concept drift that expectation mismatch concept has changed that chart board was used for some other purpose and now it is used for some other purpose so you can drift from any type so drift you cannot control I don't think you can control drift in any AI based system some form of drift gets in any other question guys if there is no\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, indicating a perfectly faithful output.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Drift can be of many types, including data drift, model drift, and concept drift.\",\n    \"Concept drift involves a mismatch between expectations and the current concept.\",\n    \"The speaker believes that drift cannot be controlled in any AI-based system.\",\n    \"User drift can occur when the demographic of platform users changes, such as from millennials to Gen Z.\",\n    \"The speaker's current offline evaluation is working fine.\",\n    \"A user who was previously a millennial is not happy with the system.\",\n    \"The speaker uses the last n conversations to perform evaluation.\",\n    \"Data drift is generally said to occur over time.\",\n    \"If input dynamics do not change, the speaker questions whether data will still drift.\",\n    \"The speaker has sampled 100 documents and the task is to query them.\",\n    \"Model drift can happen even if the data has not changed.\",\n    \"The speaker considers their example of user drift to be a very bad example.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Drift is any kind of change that makes the world your model sees look different from the world it was trained and evaluated on.\",\n    \"In the material we discussed, drift isn\u2019t limited to just one thing\u2014it can show up in several guises.\",\n    \"The most common flavors are data drift, where the actual input data start to shift; model drift, where the model\u2019s behavior itself changes over time; and concept drift, which is a mismatch between what users expect and what the model delivers.\",\n    \"There\u2019s even a kind of \u201cuser drift,\u201d where the audience using the system changes \u2013 for example, a platform that was originally popular with millennials might suddenly see a wave of Gen\u2011Z users, and their expectations could be quite different.\",\n    \"The offline evaluations you performed before launch are based on a snapshot of data and user expectations at that moment.\",\n    \"If the data, the model, the underlying concept, or the user base shifts later on, the performance you measured in the lab no longer guarantees the same experience for real users.\",\n    \"In the example from the notes, a model that was scoring well offline could start receiving thumbs\u2011downs because the new generation of users isn\u2019t getting the answers they expect.\",\n    \"That mismatch is precisely what drift captures.\",\n    \"Drift matters because it can silently erode the quality of your AI system after it\u2019s live.\",\n    \"Even if you keep the input dynamics static\u2014say you have a fixed set of 100 documents you query against\u2014other forms of drift (like changing user expectations or subtle model aging) can still appear.\",\n    \"Recognizing that drift is inevitable in any AI\u2011driven system helps you plan for ongoing monitoring and evaluation, rather than assuming that a one\u2011time offline test will hold forever.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response fully addressed the question about drift and its importance after deployment, with no irrelevant statements.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Sure, let\u2019s unpack the idea of \u201cdrift\u201d and why it becomes a real concern once your model is out in the wild.\",\n    \"Think of drift as any kind of change that makes the world your model sees look different from the world it was trained and evaluated on.\",\n    \"In the material we discussed, drift isn\u2019t limited to just one thing\u2014it can show up in several guises.\",\n    \"The most common flavors are data drift, where the actual input data start to shift; model drift, where the model\u2019s behavior itself changes over time; and concept drift, which is a mismatch between what users expect and what the model delivers.\",\n    \"There\u2019s even a kind of \u201cuser drift,\u201d where the audience using the system changes \u2013 for example, a platform that was originally popular with millennials might suddenly see a wave of Gen\u2011Z users, and their expectations could be quite different.\",\n    \"Why does any of this matter after deployment?\",\n    \"The key point is that the offline evaluations you performed before launch are based on a snapshot of data and user expectations at that moment.\",\n    \"If the data, the model, the underlying concept, or the user base shifts later on, the performance you measured in the lab no longer guarantees the same experience for real users.\",\n    \"In the example from the notes, a model that was scoring well offline could start receiving thumbs\u2011downs because the new generation of users isn\u2019t getting the answers they expect.\",\n    \"That mismatch is precisely what drift captures.\",\n    \"In short, drift matters because it can silently erode the quality of your AI system after it\u2019s live.\",\n    \"Even if you keep the input dynamics static\u2014say you have a fixed set of 100 documents you query against\u2014other forms of drift (like changing user expectations or subtle model aging) can still appear.\",\n    \"Recognizing that drift is inevitable in any AI\u2011driven system helps you plan for ongoing monitoring and evaluation, rather than assuming that a one\u2011time offline test will hold forever.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"We rely on an LLM as a judge mainly because the way an answer shows up in a model\\u2019s output can be wildly varied, and a simple \\u201cdoes this exact string appear?\\u201d check just isn\\u2019t robust enough.  \\n\\nWhen a model prints its response, it might say \\u201cthe answer is\\u202fA,\\u201d or it could embed the answer in a sentence like \\u201cA is the correct choice,\\u201d or even phrase it more indirectly. A plain string\\u2011match would only catch one exact spelling and would miss all the other legitimate ways the answer can be expressed. An LLM, on the other hand, can read the whole output, understand the surrounding language, and reliably pull out the intended answer \\u2013 in this case the letter \\u201cA\\u201d \\u2013 no matter how it\\u2019s phrased.  \\n\\nThe context also points out that early judges (for example, an early version of GPT\\u20114) sometimes failed to extract the answer correctly, which pulled the measured accuracy down. As newer, more capable LLMs have been introduced, they get better at this extraction task, raising the judge\\u2019s accuracy. This pattern\\u2014improving performance as the underlying model improves\\u2014shows why we favor a flexible, language\\u2011aware judge rather than a brittle string matcher. \\n\\nIn short, we use an LLM as a judge because it can handle the many possible textual forms an answer might take, ensuring a fair and accurate evaluation that a simple exact\\u2011match approach would simply miss.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"why do we use an llm as a judge instead of simple string matching?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"data set has become a part of their alignment training data so contamination is in that sense, not in pre-training, but in alignment stage after that disputed gold labels so here we have how many questions around 817 questions, among them there are many questions about people's disputes that this is not a misconception, this is correct so that kind of affects it and lastly deprecated GPT judge, breaks, cross or comparison I forgot to tell you this so I told you that there are three ways to measure this, one is generation where model A, B, C or D is printing or generating by itself, second is MC1 where you are doing log probabilities MC2 is you are doing log probabilities against the right ones and then joining the right ones, I told you three ways in the generated model you are getting A, B, C, D which is available to analyze this answer you use an LLM as a judge because anything can be printed here this can also be the answer is A or it can be printed as A is the answer so you have\", \"you use an LLM as a judge because anything can be printed here this can also be the answer is A or it can be printed as A is the answer so you have to extract A from all kinds of scenarios so to do this you use an LLM so when this benchmark was released in the beginning they used a version of GPT-4 as a LLM as a judge now what happened is new models came forward and people started using LLM as a judge so there was an issue in performance that the previous generation might have made a mistake in extracting A in some questions so due to that the accuracy came down but the later LLM as a judge they are better, they are able to extract each answer correctly so their accuracy is a little higher so in that sense there is a problem here, you will see this pattern whenever you are using LLM as a judge so over time since LLM's performance is improving so is the performance of LLM as a judge so due to that there is a little discrepancy today the measurement you are taking you can't compare it\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, showing the actual output perfectly aligns with the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"An LLM is used as a judge to extract answer choices such as \"A\" from various printed formats.\",\n    \"When the benchmark was first released, a version of GPT-4 was used as the LLM judge.\",\n    \"Later, newer models were adopted as LLM judges.\",\n    \"The previous generation LLM sometimes made mistakes extracting the answer, which lowered accuracy.\",\n    \"Later LLM judges are better at extracting answers correctly, resulting in slightly higher accuracy.\",\n    \"As LLM performance improves over time, the performance of LLMs used as judges also improves.\",\n    \"Because of changes in LLM judge performance, current measurements cannot be directly compared to earlier ones.\",\n    \"The dataset has become part of the alignment training data.\",\n    \"Contamination occurs during the alignment stage, not during pre\u2011training.\",\n    \"The dataset contains approximately 817 questions.\",\n    \"Many of the questions in the dataset involve people's disputes.\",\n    \"The statement that many questions involve disputes is presented as correct, not a misconception.\",\n    \"There is a deprecated GPT judge.\",\n    \"There are three ways to measure model performance: generation (models output answer choices), MC1 (log probabilities), and MC2 (log probabilities against the correct answers followed by joining the right ones).\",\n    \"In generation mode, models produce answer choices such as A, B, C, or D.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"We rely on an LLM as a judge mainly because the way an answer shows up in a model\u2019s output can be wildly varied, and a simple \u201cdoes this exact string appear?\u201d check just isn\u2019t robust enough.\",\n    \"When a model prints its response, it might say \u201cthe answer is\u202fA,\u201d or it could embed the answer in a sentence like \u201cA is the correct choice,\u201d or even phrase it more indirectly.\",\n    \"A plain string\u2011match would only catch one exact spelling and would miss all the other legitimate ways the answer can be expressed.\",\n    \"An LLM, on the other hand, can read the whole output, understand the surrounding language, and reliably pull out the intended answer \u2013 in this case the letter \u201cA\u201d \u2013 no matter how it\u2019s phrased.\",\n    \"The context also points out that early judges (for example, an early version of GPT\u20114) sometimes failed to extract the answer correctly, which pulled the measured accuracy down.\",\n    \"As newer, more capable LLMs have been introduced, they get better at this extraction task, raising the judge\u2019s accuracy.\",\n    \"This pattern\u2014improving performance as the underlying model improves\u2014shows why we favor a flexible, language\u2011aware judge rather than a brittle string matcher.\",\n    \"In short, we use an LLM as a judge because it can handle the many possible textual forms an answer might take, ensuring a fair and accurate evaluation that a simple exact\u2011match approach would simply miss.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not mention using an LLM judge because answer formats vary; the claim is not supported by the given facts.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context does not provide examples of how models phrase answers; therefore this claim is not backed by the provided information.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not discuss the limitations of plain string\\u2011match, so the claim cannot be confirmed.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"While the context notes that LLM judges extract answer choices, it does not state they can reliably handle any phrasing; the claim is not directly supported.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context mentions improved performance with newer models but does not explicitly argue that this justifies preferring a flexible, language\\u2011aware judge over a brittle string matcher.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not state that the reason for using an LLM judge is its ability to handle many textual forms; the claim is not verified.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the answer directly addressed the question about using an LLM as a judge versus simple string matching, with no irrelevant statements.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"We rely on an LLM as a judge mainly because the way an answer shows up in a model\u2019s output can be wildly varied, and a simple \u201cdoes this exact string appear?\u201d check just isn\u2019t robust enough.\",\n    \"When a model prints its response, it might say \u201cthe answer is\u202fA,\u201d or it could embed the answer in a sentence like \u201cA is the correct choice,\u201d or even phrase it more indirectly.\",\n    \"A plain string\u2011match would only catch one exact spelling and would miss all the other legitimate ways the answer can be expressed.\",\n    \"An LLM, on the other hand, can read the whole output, understand the surrounding language, and reliably pull out the intended answer \u2013 in this case the letter \u201cA\u201d \u2013 no matter how it\u2019s phrased.\",\n    \"The context also points out that early judges (for example, an early version of GPT\u20114) sometimes failed to extract the answer correctly, which pulled the measured accuracy down.\",\n    \"As newer, more capable LLMs have been introduced, they get better at this extraction task, raising the judge\u2019s accuracy.\",\n    \"This pattern\u2014improving performance as the underlying model improves\u2014shows why we favor a flexible, language\u2011aware judge rather than a brittle string matcher.\",\n    \"In short, we use an LLM as a judge because it can handle the many possible textual forms an answer might take, ensuring a fair and accurate evaluation that a simple exact\u2011match approach would simply miss.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Sure, let\\u2019s walk through what \\u201csampling\\u201d actually means in the online\\u2011evaluation pipeline and why we do it the way we do.\\n\\nFirst, think of the whole stream of user\\u2011assistant conversations as a huge pile of data. If we just grabbed a random handful of those chats, most of them would be routine, uneventful exchanges\\u2014nothing that really stresses the system. That\\u2019s fine for a very coarse\\u2011grained health check, but it won\\u2019t give us the insight we need about the spots where the model tends to slip, such as when it gets a thumbs\\u2011down rating, when a user gets frustrated, or when the dialogue drifts into sensitive topics like money, refunds, or admissions.\\n\\nBecause of that, the online\\u2011evaluation framework uses **stratified sampling**. \\u201cStratified\\u201d just means we first split the conversation log into several buckets\\u2014or strata\\u2014based on characteristics that matter to us. In practice we run a small, lightweight model over the live stream; this model tags each conversation with signals like \\u201cthumbs\\u2011down received,\\u201d \\u201cabrupt ending,\\u201d \\u201cescalation,\\u201d \\u201cre\\u2011phrasing of the same question,\\u201d or \\u201ctalking about financial matters.\\u201d Those tags define the strata.\\n\\nOnce the conversations are bucketed, we deliberately **oversample** the strata that are more likely to reveal problems. For example, we might pull a higher proportion of chats that contain a thumbs\\u2011down, that end abruptly, or that discuss refunds. Conversely, we pull fewer samples from the \\u201cnormal\\u201d bucket where everything proceeds smoothly. By adjusting the sampling rates per bucket, the final set of conversations that we send to the evaluator is enriched for the kinds of failures we care about.\\n\\nAfter this stratified sample is assembled, the evaluation component runs its metrics on each of those selected chats\\u2014checking, say, factual correctness, relevance, or safety. The resulting scores are then aggregated over a chosen time window and displayed on the monitoring dashboard. If the aggregated metric crosses a predefined threshold, an alert is raised so the team can intervene quickly.\\n\\nSo, in short, sampling in online evaluation isn\\u2019t a blind random draw; it\\u2019s a targeted, stratified process that first categorizes conversations with a small classifier, then draws more examples from the problematic categories (thumbs\\u2011down, money\\u2011related, abrupt, escalations, etc.) and fewer from the benign ones, feeds those into the evaluator, aggregates the outcomes, and finally uses the aggregated signal to drive alerts and dashboard visualizations. This approach ensures that the evaluation surface is rich in the very cases where the model is most likely to misbehave, giving you a much clearer picture of real\\u2011world performance.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how does sampling work in online evaluation?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"up more samples from those categories and less samples from normal ones. So from this the sampled conversations I will get, there is more chance of hallucination being detected. So I have discussed the same thing with you. We are doing clustering. You will have to set a small model for this. A small model will detect which one has thumbs down, which one is talking like this. So this is technicality. I just wanted to tell you that you don't do normal sampling you do stratified sampling. So is the point clear guys? So far the flow I have told you is correct. We have locked it. After that we did sampling. After that we set up our evaluator. Evaluator performs the evaluation on those sampled conversations and gives us the metric. We are aggregating that metric over a time period and showing it on the dashboard. And if a threshold is crossing then I am triggering the alerting system. This is the entire flow for online eval. There is one evaluation pipeline. You set up multiple evaluators\", \"use of keeping those conversations? We will not know that. What about we focus more on those questions where we got thumbs down? Will it be a good strategy? Think about it. Will it be a good strategy if we focus more on those questions where we got thumbs down? Right? Or we focus on those conversations which suddenly got over abruptly or where escalations happened. Or where I don't know the user is rephrasing the same question in different ways. Or where we are talking about money, refund, admission, fees. So basically what I am trying to say is that not all conversations are the same. What you do is you do stratified sampling. Stratified sampling. You basically divide all your conversations into categories. And then you pick up more samples in problematic categories. Like we are talking about money or thumbs down. So I will pick up more samples from those categories and less samples from normal ones. So from this the sampled conversations I will get, there is more chance of\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, showing the actual output perfectly aligns with the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"The described process uses stratified sampling instead of normal sampling.\",\n    \"A small model is used to detect which conversations have thumbs down.\",\n    \"An evaluator performs evaluation on sampled conversations and provides a metric.\",\n    \"Metrics are aggregated over a time period and shown on a dashboard.\",\n    \"An alerting system is triggered when a threshold is crossed.\",\n    \"There is one evaluation pipeline, but multiple evaluators can be set up.\",\n    \"Conversations are divided into categories for stratified sampling.\",\n    \"More samples are taken from problematic categories such as money, refunds, admission, fees, or thumbs down, and fewer samples from normal categories.\",\n    \"Taking more samples from those categories increases the chance of detecting hallucination.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"The online\u2011evaluation framework uses stratified sampling.\",\n    \"Stratified sampling means we first split the conversation log into several buckets\u2014or strata\u2014based on characteristics that matter to us.\",\n    \"In practice we run a small, lightweight model over the live stream; this model tags each conversation with signals like \u201cthumbs\u2011down received,\u201d \u201cabrupt ending,\u201d \u201cescalation,\u201d \u201cre\u2011phrasing of the same question,\u201d or \u201ctalking about financial matters.\u201d\",\n    \"Those tags define the strata.\",\n    \"We deliberately oversample the strata that are more likely to reveal problems.\",\n    \"We might pull a higher proportion of chats that contain a thumbs\u2011down, that end abruptly, or that discuss refunds.\",\n    \"We pull fewer samples from the \u201cnormal\u201d bucket where everything proceeds smoothly.\",\n    \"By adjusting the sampling rates per bucket, the final set of conversations that we send to the evaluator is enriched for the kinds of failures we care about.\",\n    \"After this stratified sample is assembled, the evaluation component runs its metrics on each of those selected chats\u2014checking, say, factual correctness, relevance, or safety.\",\n    \"The resulting scores are then aggregated over a chosen time window and displayed on the monitoring dashboard.\",\n    \"If the aggregated metric crosses a predefined threshold, an alert is raised so the team can intervene quickly.\",\n    \"Sampling in online evaluation isn\u2019t a blind random draw; it\u2019s a targeted, stratified process that first categorizes conversations with a small classifier, then draws more examples from the problematic categories (thumbs\u2011down, money\u2011related, abrupt, escalations, etc.) and fewer from the benign ones, feeds those into the evaluator, aggregates the outcomes, and finally uses the aggregated signal to drive alerts and dashboard visualizations.\",\n    \"This approach ensures that the evaluation surface is rich in the very cases where the model is most likely to misbehave, giving you a much clearer picture of real\u2011world performance.\",\n    \"If we just grabbed a random handful of those chats, most of them would be routine, uneventful exchanges\u2014nothing that really stresses the system.\",\n    \"That\u2019s fine for a very coarse\u2011grained health check, but it won\u2019t give us the insight we need about the spots where the model tends to slip, such as when it gets a thumbs\u2011down rating, when a user gets frustrated, or when the dialogue drifts into sensitive topics like money, refunds, or admissions.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context only mentions a small model detecting thumbs\\u2011down conversations; it does not mention tagging for abrupt endings, escalations, re\\u2011phrasing, or financial matters.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context confirms oversampling of thumbs\\u2011down and refund\\u2011related chats, but it does not mention oversampling of chats that end abruptly.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context states that an evaluator provides a metric for sampled conversations, but it does not specify that the metrics check factual correctness, relevance, or safety.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"While the context describes stratified sampling with a small classifier and oversampling of problematic categories (thumbs\\u2011down, money\\u2011related), it does not mention categories such as abrupt endings or escalations, so those parts are unsupported.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context does not make any claim about this approach guaranteeing a richer evaluation surface or a clearer picture of real\\u2011world performance.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context does not discuss what would happen if a random handful of chats were selected, so this claim is not supported.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context does not evaluate the usefulness of random sampling for coarse\\u2011grained health checks versus stratified sampling, so this claim is not backed by the provided information.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response directly and completely answered the question about how sampling works in online evaluation, with no irrelevant statements.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"We will walk through what sampling actually means in the online\u2011evaluation pipeline and why we do it the way we do.\",\n    \"The whole stream of user\u2011assistant conversations can be thought of as a huge pile of data.\",\n    \"If we just grabbed a random handful of those chats, most of them would be routine, uneventful exchanges that do not stress the system.\",\n    \"That approach is fine for a coarse\u2011grained health check but it won\u2019t give us insight about spots where the model tends to slip, such as thumbs\u2011down ratings, user frustration, or dialogues that drift into sensitive topics like money, refunds, or admissions.\",\n    \"Because of that, the online\u2011evaluation framework uses stratified sampling.\",\n    \"Stratified sampling means we first split the conversation log into several buckets\u2014or strata\u2014based on characteristics that matter to us.\",\n    \"In practice we run a small, lightweight model over the live stream that tags each conversation with signals like \"thumbs\u2011down received,\" \"abrupt ending,\" \"escalation,\" \"re\u2011phrasing of the same question,\" or \"talking about financial matters.\"\",\n    \"Those tags define the strata.\",\n    \"Once the conversations are bucketed, we deliberately oversample the strata that are more likely to reveal problems.\",\n    \"For example, we might pull a higher proportion of chats that contain a thumbs\u2011down, end abruptly, or discuss refunds.\",\n    \"Conversely, we pull fewer samples from the normal bucket where everything proceeds smoothly.\",\n    \"By adjusting the sampling rates per bucket, the final set of conversations sent to the evaluator is enriched for the kinds of failures we care about.\",\n    \"After this stratified sample is assembled, the evaluation component runs its metrics on each selected chat, checking factual correctness, relevance, or safety.\",\n    \"The resulting scores are then aggregated over a chosen time window and displayed on the monitoring dashboard.\",\n    \"If the aggregated metric crosses a predefined threshold, an alert is raised so the team can intervene quickly.\",\n    \"Sampling in online evaluation isn\u2019t a blind random draw; it is a targeted, stratified process that first categorizes conversations with a small classifier, then draws more examples from problematic categories and fewer from benign ones, feeds those into the evaluator, aggregates the outcomes, and finally uses the aggregated signal to drive alerts and dashboard visualizations.\",\n    \"This approach ensures that the evaluation surface is rich in the cases where the model is most likely to misbehave, giving a clearer picture of real\u2011world performance.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"In an online evaluation you\\u2019ll want to keep track of two different kinds of feedback that your system can collect: **computed signals** and **captured signals**.\\n\\nA **captured signal** is something that the user or the environment already gives you directly, so you don\\u2019t have to do any extra work to derive it. You simply record it as it arrives. The classic examples are the thumbs\\u2011up or thumbs\\u2011down buttons that a user might click after a response\\u2014those clicks are explicit indications of satisfaction or dissatisfaction, and you just store that value in your logging system (for instance, LangSmith). Latency is another captured signal: once the model produces an answer you can measure how long it took, and you log that duration straight away. The same goes for cost\\u2011per\\u2011conversation metrics such as the number of tokens used or the monetary cost incurred; these are reported by the system and can be recorded without any further calculation.\\n\\nA **computed signal**, by contrast, is something you have to infer or calculate yourself before you can log it. The context you provided doesn\\u2019t give a concrete example of a computed signal, but the distinction is that you would need to apply some processing\\u2014perhaps aggregating multiple raw observations, applying a formula, or deriving a metric from the raw data\\u2014to turn the underlying information into a signal you can store. In other words, computed signals require an extra step of calculation, whereas captured signals are already in a ready\\u2011to\\u2011store form.\\n\\nSo, in short, captured signals are directly observed data like thumbs\\u2011up/down, latency, token count, or monetary cost that you log as\\u2011is, while computed signals are metrics you must derive through additional processing before they can be recorded.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what are computed and captured signals in online eval?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"not? Okay? So there are actually two types of signals which you have to focus on. The first one is computed and the second one is captured signals. Okay? So computed signals are those that you have to calculate or figure out. And captured signals are those that are already present and you have to store them somewhere. I will give you both the examples. For example, captured signal is thumbs up, thumbs down. If the user did thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then this is a signal that the user is giving me. So I am directly storing it in the langsmith. Now I didn't need to calculate anything here. The user said thumbs down, I have to store it as it is. So we call this captured signal. One more example is latency. To do any particular answer, how much time did my chatbot take? Whatever time it took, I have to store it in the langsmith. Now I don't need to calculate anything here. Cost per conversation. How much token did I spend? How much money did I spend? Again, it is\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, indicating perfect faithfulness to the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"There are two types of signals: computed signals and captured signals.\",\n    \"Computed signals are signals that you have to calculate or figure out.\",\n    \"Captured signals are signals that are already present and you have to store them somewhere.\",\n    \"An example of a captured signal is a thumbs up or thumbs down given by the user during a conversation.\",\n    \"When a user gives a thumbs up or thumbs down, the signal is stored directly in LangSmith without any calculation.\",\n    \"Latency, the amount of time a chatbot takes to provide an answer, is treated as a captured signal.\",\n    \"The latency value is stored in LangSmith without needing to calculate anything.\",\n    \"Cost per conversation, such as the number of tokens spent or the amount of money spent, is considered a captured signal.\",\n    \"The cost per conversation is stored in LangSmith without calculation.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"In an online evaluation you\u2019ll want to keep track of two different kinds of feedback that your system can collect: computed signals and captured signals.\",\n    \"A captured signal is something that the user or the environment already gives you directly, so you don\u2019t have to do any extra work to deri