he scores are pretty low. In the early days you might see numbers like 25\\u202f% or 36\\u202f% accuracy because the models are still learning how to handle those kinds of problems.\\n\\nNow, language models get updated every few months. Each new version gets a little better, so over time the same test starts to look easier to the models. After a while, the scores climb: the next generation might hit 50\\u202f%, then 70\\u202f%, and eventually you\\u2019ll see most of the leading models scoring in the mid\\u201190s. At that point, the scores of different systems\\u2014Opus\\u202f9.5 at 95\\u202f%, GPT\\u202f5.6 at 94\\u202f%, Google Gemini at 92\\u202f%\\u2014are all clustered very tightly together.\\n\\nWhen this happens, the benchmark has become \\u201csaturated.\\u201d The word \\u201csaturated\\u201d here just means that the test is fully \\u201cfilled up\\u201d with high scores; it no longer spreads the models apart. Because every model is getting almost the same mark, the benchmark stops being useful for telling us which model is better or for measuring real progress. It\\u2019s like an exam where everyone gets an A\\u2014if you can\\u2019t differentiate performance, the test no longer serves its purpose.\\n\\nWhen a benchmark reaches this saturation point, the community typically retires it and creates a new, more challenging benchmark. The life cycle looks like this: a fresh benchmark is released \\u2192 models struggle at first \\u2192 they improve and eventually all score near\\u2011perfectly \\u2192 the benchmark is declared saturated and is replaced by a new one. That\\u2019s why, for example, the GSM\\u20118K and MMLU benchmarks are now considered saturated: most current models are already achieving scores in the mid\\u201190s, so they don\\u2019t help us compare new systems any more.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what does it mean for a benchmark to get saturated?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"t