ity of a conversation. Each of those has its own evaluation pipeline. For example, if you want to set up a pipeline for hallucination, you would click on the hallucination option, give the pipeline a name, choose the application you\\u2019re testing, and then select a judge model (like OpenAI or Claude) to score the outputs.\\n\\nSo the key difference is that safety pipelines (like the one for toxicity) are built around detecting harmful content, whereas quality pipelines are built around measuring how accurate, coherent, or useful the model\\u2019s responses are. Both pipelines let you pick the application you\\u2019re testing and the judge model that will perform the scoring, but the underlying metrics, test cases, and even the judges you might choose can differ because the objectives are distinct: preventing harm versus ensuring high\\u2011quality performance.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how are safety issues like toxicity evaluated differently from quality issues?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"detecting. Code injection is different for detecting. In safety it is different for toxicity. It is different for bias and fairness. Talking about quality hallucination has a different evaluation pipeline. Correctness is different. Assertions, conciseness code checker. The quality of conversation is different. Trajectory is actually for agent. It is not for chat bots. It is for agents. It is different for image based chat bots. It is different for voice based chat bots. If you click on a particular one. Like we were talking about hallucination. If you want to set up a pipeline for hallucination then you click on it. Here you will give it a name. From here you will select the application on which you want to run this evaluation. Obviously there can be more than one applications in your organization. Assume there is also one application here. After that you will select from here that here which model you want to use in LLM as a judge. From here you will select open AI or Claude or\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, showing perfect alignment between the actual output and the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Code injection detection is different from detecting other issues.\",\n    \"In safety, detection is different for toxicity.\",\n    \"Detection is different for bias and fairness.\",\n    \"Hallucination evaluation has a different pipeline.\",\n    \"Correctness evaluation is different.\",\n    \"Assertions, conciseness, and code checking are separate quality aspects.\",\n    \"The quality of conversation is evaluated differently.\",\n    \"Trajectory evaluation is for agents, not for chat bots.\",\n    \"Trajectory evaluation is not for image-based chat bots.\",\n    \"Trajectory evaluation is not for voice-based chat bots.\",\n    \"To set up a hallucination pipeline, you click on the hallucination option.\",\n    \"When setting up a hallucination pipeline, you give it a name.\",\n    \"You select the application on which to run the evaluation.\",\n    \"An organization can have more than one application.\",\n    \"You select which model to use as a judge in the LLM.\",\n    \"Model options for the judge include OpenAI and Claude.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"First, think of safety and quality as two separate \u201cdomains\u201d of evaluation.\",\n    \"When you\u2019re looking at safety, the system\u2019s goal is to catch things that could be harmful or inappropriate, such as hateful language, offensive remarks, or biased statements.\",\n    \"The course material tells us that \u201cin safety it is different for toxicity\u201d and that toxicity is also treated separately from other safety dimensions like bias and fairness.\",\n    \"In practice, that means you would pick a safety\u2011focused evaluation pipeline that is specifically tuned to detect those kinds of problematic outputs.\",\n    \"Quality issues, on the other hand, are about how well the model performs its intended task.\",\n    \"The material mentions several quality\u2011related dimensions\u2014hallucination (producing false or fabricated information), correctness (getting facts right), conciseness, code correctness, and the overall quality of a conversation.\",\n    \"Each of those has its own evaluation pipeline.\",\n    \"For example, if you want to set up a pipeline for hallucination, you would click on the hallucination option, give the pipeline a name, choose the application you\u2019re testing, and then select a judge model (like OpenAI or Claude) to score the outputs.\",\n    \"So the key difference is that safety pipelines (like the one for toxicity) are built around detecting harmful content, whereas quality pipelines are built around measuring how accurate, coherent, or useful the model\u2019s responses are.\",\n    \"Both pipelines let you pick the application you\u2019re testing and the judge model that will perform the scoring, but the underlying metrics, test cases, and even the judges you might choose can differ because the objectives are distinct: preventing harm versus ensuring high\u2011quality performance.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response fully addressed the question with no irrelevant content.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Sure, let\u2019s walk through how the evaluation of safety concerns\u2014like toxicity\u2014gets set up differently from the way we evaluate quality\u2011related problems.\",\n    \"Think of safety and quality as two separate \u201cdomains\u201d of evaluation.\",\n    \"When evaluating safety, the system\u2019s goal is to catch things that could be harmful or inappropriate, such as hateful language, offensive remarks, or biased statements.\",\n    \"The course material says that \u201cin safety it is different for toxicity\u201d and that toxicity is treated separately from other safety dimensions like bias and fairness.\",\n    \"In practice, you would pick a safety\u2011focused evaluation pipeline specifically tuned to detect those problematic outputs.\",\n    \"Quality issues are about how well the model performs its intended task.\",\n    \"The material mentions several quality\u2011related dimensions: hallucination, correctness, conciseness, code correctness, and overall conversation quality.\",\n    \"Each quality dimension has its own evaluation pipeline.\",\n    \"To set up a pipeline for hallucination, you would click the hallucination option, give the pipeline a name, choose the application you\u2019re testing, and select a judge model (like OpenAI or Claude) to score the outputs.\",\n    \"The key difference is that safety pipelines (like the one for toxicity) are built around detecting harmful content, whereas quality pipelines are built around measuring how accurate, coherent, or useful the model\u2019s responses are.\",\n    \"Both pipelines let you pick the application you\u2019re testing and the judge model that will perform the scoring, but the underlying metrics, test cases, and judges can differ because the objectives are distinct: preventing harm versus ensuring high\u2011quality performance.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Think of knowledge capability as the amount of world facts that end up stored inside an LLM\\u2019s weights after training. The idea behind that capability is that when we first trained large language models on a massive, internet\\u2011scale corpus, we expected the models to be able to answer virtually any question you could find online. In other words, the sheer volume of data was seen as the source of the model\\u2019s breadth of knowledge.\\n\\nThat expectation, however, doesn\\u2019t mean that simply adding more data will automatically make a model more knowledgeable in every case. The benchmark we use to gauge \\u201chow smart\\u201d a model is\\u2014MMLU\\u2014focuses on breadth across many subjects, and it was created precisely because we needed a way to measure what the model has actually learned, not just how much data it has seen. From the context we know that the benchmark evaluates knowledge across 57 subjects and 14\\u202f000 multiple\\u2011choice questions drawn from real exams and textbooks. The fact that we need such a test tells us that more data alone isn\\u2019t a guarantee of better performance; we still have to check whether the model can retrieve and apply that knowledge correctly.\\n\\nSo, training on a larger, diverse dataset generally gives a model more material to absorb, which can increase its knowledge capability, but it isn\\u2019t a universal rule. The ultimate measure is how well the model performs on evaluations like MMLU, which look at the breadth (and indirectly the depth) of what the model actually knows. In short, more data can help, but other factors\\u2014such as the model\\u2019s architecture, the quality and variety of the data, and how the training is conducted\\u2014also play crucial roles in determining how knowledgeable the final LLM becomes.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"does training on more data always make an llm more knowledgeable?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"start our discussion around the knowledge capability ok now first of all I will explain the whole evolution that how the evolution of benchmarks happened in knowledge capability with the help of a flow chart and once you understand the flow chart then we will cover the most important benchmarks in this capability one by one that's the idea of this class so first of all let's talk about knowledge capability knowledge capability simply tells that when you train an LLM then how much knowledge he was able to retain from his training process basically how much world knowledge is hidden in his weights and biases his parametric knowledge is a way to test knowledge capability and this is the most important capability think about it when the LLMs were trained for the first time on this massive internet scale data then the expectation would be that if we ask about anything on the internet then it will tell us so knowledge capability is like the most fundamental capability that an LLM can have\", \"write yes we will start ok so yeah, first of all we will discuss MMLU because this is the mother of all benchmarks at least in knowledge capability this is the mother of all benchmarks this is where things originated, so as I said the specialty of this benchmark is breadth of knowledge this simply tests one thing of LLM that how much breadth of knowledge it has not depth of knowledge, but breadth of knowledge and to do this in this benchmark over 57 subjects, 14000 multiple choice questions data set was made and from where this data set was taken where these questions have come from, real exams USMLE, USGRE, AP and other people have also sourced from their side now obviously it must have been taken from the internet from the text book, but yeah the main idea is that 57 subjects, 14000 questions MCQs ok, it was launched in September 2020 and it had only one job to tell how smart is a given LLM, smart in terms of how much knowledge it has, ok from 2021 to 2024 all the LLMs that have\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Knowledge capability refers to how much knowledge an LLM retains from its training process.\",\n    \"Knowledge capability measures the amount of world knowledge hidden in an LLM's weights and biases.\",\n    \"Parametric knowledge is a way to test knowledge capability.\",\n    \"Knowledge capability is considered the most important capability of an LLM.\",\n    \"When LLMs were first trained on massive internet-scale data, the expectation was that they could answer any internet-based question.\",\n    \"Knowledge capability is described as the most fundamental capability that an LLM can have.\",\n    \"MMLU is described as the \"mother of all benchmarks\" in knowledge capability.\",\n    \"MMLU tests the breadth of knowledge of an LLM, not depth.\",\n    \"The MMLU benchmark contains questions over 57 subjects.\",\n    \"The MMLU dataset includes 14,000 multiple-choice questions.\",\n    \"MMLU questions are sourced from real exams such as USMLE, USGRE, AP, and other sources.\",\n    \"The MMLU benchmark was launched in September 2020.\",\n    \"The purpose of MMLU is to assess how much knowledge a given LLM has.\",\n    \"From 2021 to 2024, LLMs have been evaluated using the MMLU benchmark.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Think of knowledge capability as the amount of world facts that end up stored inside an LLM\u2019s weights after training.\",\n    \"The idea behind that capability is that when we first trained large language models on a massive, internet\u2011scale corpus, we expected the models to be able to answer virtually any question you could find online.\",\n    \"In other words, the sheer volume of data was seen as the source of the model\u2019s breadth of knowledge.\",\n    \"That expectation, however, doesn\u2019t mean that simply adding more data will automatically make a model more knowledgeable in every case.\",\n    \"The benchmark we use to gauge \u201chow smart\u201d a model is\u2014MMLU\u2014focuses on breadth across many subjects, and it was created precisely because we needed a way to measure what the model has actually learned, not just how much data it has seen.\",\n    \"From the context we know that the benchmark evaluates knowledge across 57 subjects and 14\u202f000 multiple\u2011choice questions drawn from real exams and textbooks.\",\n    \"The fact that we need such a test tells us that more data alone isn\u2019t a guarantee of better performance; we still have to check whether the model can retrieve and apply that knowledge correctly.\",\n    \"So, training on a larger, diverse dataset generally gives a model more material to absorb, which can increase its knowledge capability, but it isn\u2019t a universal rule.\",\n    \"The ultimate measure is how well the model performs on evaluations like MMLU, which look at the breadth (and indirectly the depth) of what the model actually knows.\",\n    \"In short, more data can help, but other factors\u2014such as the model\u2019s architecture, the quality and variety of the data, and how the training is conducted\u2014also play crucial roles in determining how knowledgeable the final LLM becomes.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not explicitly state that the volume of data was seen as the source of the model\\u2019s breadth of knowledge, so the claim cannot be confirmed.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context contains no information about whether adding more data always improves knowledge, so the claim is not supported nor contradicted.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not discuss the relationship between data quantity and performance guarantees, so this claim is not verifiable from the given information.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not address whether training on a larger, diverse dataset universally increases knowledge capability, so the claim cannot be confirmed.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not mention model architecture, data quality, or training procedures, so the claim is not supported by the provided information.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response fully addressed the question about whether training on more data always makes an LLM more knowledgeable, with no irrelevant statements.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Knowledge capability is the amount of world facts stored in an LLM\u2019s weights after training.\",\n    \"When large language models were first trained on a massive internet\u2011scale corpus, it was expected they could answer virtually any online question.\",\n    \"The sheer volume of data was seen as the source of a model\u2019s breadth of knowledge.\",\n    \"Adding more data does not automatically make a model more knowledgeable in every case.\",\n    \"The benchmark MMLU gauges how smart a model is by focusing on breadth across many subjects.\",\n    \"MMLU was created to measure what the model has actually learned, not just how much data it has seen.\",\n    \"The benchmark evaluates knowledge across 57 subjects and 14,000 multiple\u2011choice questions from real exams and textbooks.\",\n    \"The need for such a test indicates that more data alone isn\u2019t a guarantee of better performance.\",\n    \"Models must be checked for their ability to retrieve and apply knowledge correctly.\",\n    \"Training on a larger, diverse dataset generally gives a model more material to absorb, which can increase its knowledge capability, but it isn\u2019t a universal rule.\",\n    \"The ultimate measure is how well the model performs on evaluations like MMLU, which assess breadth and indirectly depth of knowledge.\",\n    \"More data can help, but other factors\u2014model architecture, data quality and variety, and training methods\u2014also play crucial roles in determining a final LLM\u2019s knowledgeability.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"When you put a chatbot into production, the most useful logs are the ones that let you reconstruct exactly what happened in each turn and also give you enough data to spot problems or improve the system later.\\u202fIn plain language, think of a log as a diary entry for every interaction: you want to record not just what the user said and what the bot replied, but also the \\u201cbehind\\u2011the\\u2011scenes\\u201d details that tell you how the request was processed.\\n\\nFirst, capture the user\\u2019s input and the exact question you asked the retrieval\\u2011augmented generation (RAG) system.\\u202fThat way you know what the user was trying to get at, and you can later compare it with the context that the system pulled in order to answer.\\u202fNext, store the context that the RAG component fetched \\u2013 essentially the set of documents or snippets that were fed to the language model to produce the answer.\\u202fTogether with the context, keep the model\\u2019s output (the response that was sent back to the user).\\u202fHaving the three pieces\\u2014user query, retrieved context, and bot answer\\u2014lets you replay any conversation and understand why a particular response was generated.\\n\\nBeyond the content of the conversation, it\\u2019s important to log operational metrics.\\u202fLatency in milliseconds tells you how long the request took, which is useful for performance monitoring.\\u202fToken counts\\u2014how many tokens were used in the prompt and how many were generated as completion\\u2014let you track usage and cost, and you should also record the total monetary cost of the call.\\u202fIf anything went wrong, capture the error information, including the status code, so you can diagnose failures quickly.\\n\\nFinally, don\\u2019t forget the behavioral signals that give you a sense of the user\\u2019s satisfaction or escalation.\\u202fIf the user clicks a thumbs\\u2011up or thumbs\\u2011down, logs that reaction.\\u202fIf the user asks to speak to a human, provides an email address, or otherwise indicates that the conversation is escalating, record that intent.\\u202fRepeated or re\\u2011phrased questions are also worth noting, because they often signal that the bot isn\\u2019t meeting the user\\u2019s needs.\\n\\nIn short, a good production log should include:\\n\\n* the user\\u2019s original question,\\n* the retrieved context the bot used,\\n* the bot\\u2019s response,\\n* performance data such as latency and token usage,\\n* cost information,\\n* any error codes if the request failed,\\n* user feedback signals (thumbs up/down, escalation requests, email contacts), and\\n* patterns like repeated or re\\u2011phrased queries.\\n\\nTools like Langsmith already provide a convenient place to store all of these pieces together, organizing them by project so you can browse full conversation histories, see the metadata, and run analyses later. By capturing each of these elements, you\\u2019ll have a complete picture of what happened in production and the data you need to improve the system over time.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what information should i store when logging production conversations?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"store the question that the user asked. What context was generated in the rag chat bot in the process of answering that question? I will store that context in the chat bot. I will store the output that the chat bot gave on the basis of that context and question. Apart from that, I will store a lot of operational things such as latency in milliseconds, how many tokens were spent in the prompt, how many completion tokens are there, how much total cost was spent, did any error come? If an error came, what is its status code? I will store all this. Apart from this, if the user is giving me some other signals, I will store those signals too. Like if there is a behavioral metric such as he is pressing thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then I will store that too. Is he mailing? He is saying that I don't want to talk to you, I want to talk to a human or give my email ID, I want to mail. So if the matter is escalating, I will store that. The user is asking the same questions\", \"want to talk to a human or give my email ID, I want to mail. So if the matter is escalating, I will store that. The user is asking the same questions again and again because he feels that the chat bot is not helping him, he is rephrasing the question. So I will store all these things. Everything will be stored. Can someone tell me a tool to store all this? Have you seen a tool in the past where you use that tool to store such things? Perfect. Amin was right. I had also taught on YouTube, we had covered in detail that we had made a chat bot and we were storing every conversation of that chat bot. Where? On Langsmith. I can show you right now. So see this. This is Langsmith. Langsmith has different projects. What can you do in those projects? All your conversations are there. These are all conversations. You can store those conversations. You store everything in it. What the user sent, what was your output, there is a lot of metadata. Everything is stored. So this is kept with you. Now\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, indicating the actual output fully aligns with the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"The user wants to store the question that the user asked.\",\n    \"The user wants to store the context generated in the RAG chatbot during the answering process.\",\n    \"The user wants to store the output that the chatbot gave based on that context and question.\",\n    \"The user wants to store operational data such as latency in milliseconds.\",\n    \"The user wants to store the number of tokens spent in the prompt.\",\n    \"The user wants to store the number of completion tokens.\",\n    \"The user wants to store the total cost spent.\",\n    \"The user wants to store whether any error occurred.\",\n    \"If an error occurred, the user wants to store its status code.\",\n    \"The user wants to store other user signals, such as thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation.\",\n    \"The user wants to store signals indicating the user wants to talk to a human or provide an email ID, indicating escalation.\",\n    \"The user wants to store repeated questions when the user feels the chatbot is not helping and rephrases the question.\",\n    \"The user asks for a tool to store all of this information.\",\n    \"The user mentions having seen a tool in the past used to store such things.\",\n    \"The user states that Langsmith is the tool used to store every conversation of the chatbot.\",\n    \"Langsmith has different projects.\",\n    \"In Langsmith projects, all conversations can be stored.\",\n    \"Langsmith stores what the user sent, the chatbot's output, and a lot of metadata.\",\n    \"Everything is stored in Langsmith.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"When you put a chatbot into production, the most useful logs are the ones that let you reconstruct exactly what happened in each turn and also give you enough data to spot problems or improve the system later.\",\n    \"In plain language, think of a log as a diary entry for every interaction: you want to record not just what the user said and what the bot replied, but also the \u201cbehind\u2011the\u2011scenes\u201d details that tell you how the request was processed.\",\n    \"First, capture the user\u2019s input and the exact question you asked the retrieval\u2011augmented generation (RAG) system.\",\n    \"That way you know what the user was trying to get at, and you can later compare it with the context that the system pulled in order to answer.\",\n    \"Next, store the context that the RAG component fetched \u2013 essentially the set of documents or snippets that were fed to the language model to produce the answer.\",\n    \"Together with the context, keep the model\u2019s output (the response that was sent back to the user).\",\n    \"Having the three pieces\u2014user query, retrieved context, and bot answer\u2014lets you replay any conversation and understand why a particular response was generated.\",\n    \"Beyond the content of the conversation, it\u2019s important to log operational metrics.\",\n    \"Latency in milliseconds tells you how long the request took, which is useful for performance monitoring.\",\n    \"Token counts\u2014how many tokens were used in the prompt and how many were generated as completion\u2014let you track usage and cost, and you should also record the total monetary cost of the call.\",\n    \"If anything went wrong, capture the error information, including the status code, so you can diagnose failures quickly.\",\n    \"Finally, don\u2019t forget the behavioral signals that give you a sense of the user\u2019s satisfaction or escalation.\",\n    \"If the user clicks a thumbs\u2011up or thumbs\u2011down, logs that reaction.\",\n    \"If the user asks to speak to a human, provides an email address, or otherwise indicates that the conversation is escalating, record that intent.\",\n    \"Repeated or re\u2011phrased questions are also worth noting, because they often signal that the bot isn\u2019t meeting the user\u2019s needs.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include: the user\u2019s original question, the retrieved context the bot used, the bot\u2019s response, performance data such as latency and token usage, cost information, any error codes if the request failed, user feedback signals (thumbs up/down, escalation requests, email contacts), and patterns like repeated or re\u2011phrased queries.\",\n    \"Tools like Langsmith already provide a convenient place to store all of these pieces together, organizing them by project so you can browse full conversation histories, see the metadata, and run analyses later.\",\n    \"By capturing each of these elements, you\u2019ll have a complete picture of what happened in production and the data you need to improve the system over time.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response directly answered the question about what information to store when logging production conversations, with no irrelevant statements at all.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"When you put a chatbot into production, the most useful logs are the ones that let you reconstruct exactly what happened in each turn and also give you enough data to spot problems or improve the system later.\",\n    \"In plain language, think of a log as a diary entry for every interaction: you want to record not just what the user said and what the bot replied, but also the \u201cbehind\u2011the\u2011scenes\u201d details that tell you how the request was processed.\",\n    \"First, capture the user\u2019s input and the exact question you asked the retrieval\u2011augmented generation (RAG) system.\",\n    \"That way you know what the user was trying to get at, and you can later compare it with the context that the system pulled in order to answer.\",\n    \"Next, store the context that the RAG component fetched \u2013 essentially the set of documents or snippets that were fed to the language model to produce the answer.\",\n    \"Together with the context, keep the model\u2019s output (the response that was sent back to the user).\",\n    \"Having the three pieces\u2014user query, retrieved context, and bot answer\u2014lets you replay any conversation and understand why a particular response was generated.\",\n    \"Beyond the content of the conversation, it\u2019s important to log operational metrics.\",\n    \"Latency in milliseconds tells you how long the request took, which is useful for performance monitoring.\",\n    \"Token counts\u2014how many tokens were used in the prompt and how many were generated as completion\u2014let you track usage and cost, and you should also record the total monetary cost of the call.\",\n    \"If anything went wrong, capture the error information, including the status code, so you can diagnose failures quickly.\",\n    \"Finally, don\u2019t forget the behavioral signals that give you a sense of the user\u2019s satisfaction or escalation.\",\n    \"If the user clicks a thumbs\u2011up or thumbs\u2011down, logs that reaction.\",\n    \"If the user asks to speak to a human, provides an email address, or otherwise indicates that the conversation is escalating, record that intent.\",\n    \"Repeated or re\u2011phrased questions are also worth noting, because they often signal that the bot isn\u2019t meeting the user\u2019s needs.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include the user\u2019s original question.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include the retrieved context the bot used.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include the bot\u2019s response.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include performance data such as latency and token usage.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include cost information.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include any error codes if the request failed.\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include user feedback signals (thumbs up/down, escalation requests, email contacts).\",\n    \"In short, a good production log should include patterns like repeated or re\u2011phrased queries.\",\n    \"Tools like Langsmith already provide a convenient place to store all of these pieces together, organizing them by project so you can browse full conversation histories, see the metadata, and run analyses later.\",\n    \"By capturing each of these elements, you\u2019ll have a complete picture of what happened in production and the data you need to improve the system over time.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Think of the thumbs\\u2011up and thumbs\\u2011down buttons as a very lightweight way for users to tell us whether the answer they just got was right or wrong.\\u202fWhen a user clicks thumbs up, we can treat that as a positive signal saying \\u201cthis response was correct in this particular context.\\u201d\\u202fWhen they click thumbs down, it\\u2019s a negative signal saying \\u201csomething went off\\u2011track here.\\u201d\\u202fBecause we already capture a lot of surrounding information\\u2014\\u200bthe original user question, the retrieved context the RAG system used, the exact output we generated, latency, token counts, any error codes, and even other behavioral cues\\u2014\\u200bthe thumbs feedback becomes a label that we can attach to a fully documented interaction.\\n\\nBy collecting many of those labeled interactions over time, we can start to assemble a \\u201cgolden\\u201d dataset: a set of examples where we know, with high confidence, which responses are good and which are bad. The positive (thumbs\\u2011up) examples give us high\\u2011quality, correct answer\\u2013context pairs that we can use as reference or training material. The negative (thumbs\\u2011down) examples flag responses that need to be examined, corrected, or removed, helping us prune noisy or incorrect data. In short, the thumbs signals let us automatically annotate large volumes of chat logs with correctness information, and that annotation is exactly what we need to build a reliable, curated golden dataset for future evaluation or model improvement.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how can thumbs up and thumbs down signals help build a golden dataset?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"not? Is there any other signal that can tell this instead of the correct answer? Let me tell you. What if you have given the option of thumbs up and thumbs down in your chatbot? If you suddenly start seeing that in the last one hour, the rating of thumbs down is coming in a lot of conversations, then what will you understand from this? That something or the other has gone wrong. Something or the other is giving the wrong answer to our chatbot. So again, this is a tricky way to find out that is the correctness working properly or not? Yes, basically the user's feedback has been done. So here the correctness has been alternated by the user's feedback. Yeah Aman, we judge LLM as a judge. I will show you now. Look, understand one thing. One important thing is that the questions you are asking are a little ahead of the questions. Because we have not discussed the how part of online evals yet. Technically, we just discussed that why do we need online evals and what exactly are online evals?\", \"store the question that the user asked. What context was generated in the rag chat bot in the process of answering that question? I will store that context in the chat bot. I will store the output that the chat bot gave on the basis of that context and question. Apart from that, I will store a lot of operational things such as latency in milliseconds, how many tokens were spent in the prompt, how many completion tokens are there, how much total cost was spent, did any error come? If an error came, what is its status code? I will store all this. Apart from this, if the user is giving me some other signals, I will store those signals too. Like if there is a behavioral metric such as he is pressing thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then I will store that too. Is he mailing? He is saying that I don't want to talk to you, I want to talk to a human or give my email ID, I want to mail. So if the matter is escalating, I will store that. The user is asking the same questions\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, indicating the actual output fully aligns with the retrieval context.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Thumbs down feedback can indicate that a chatbot is providing incorrect answers.\",\n    \"User feedback can be used to assess the correctness of a chatbot's responses.\",\n    \"The conversation mentions the concept of online evaluations for language models.\",\n    \"Online evaluations are discussed in terms of why they are needed and what they are.\",\n    \"The system stores the question that the user asked.\",\n    \"The system stores the context generated by the RAG chatbot when answering the question.\",\n    \"The system stores the output that the chatbot provides based on the context and question.\",\n    \"Operational metrics such as latency in milliseconds are recorded.\",\n    \"The number of tokens used in the prompt is recorded.\",\n    \"The number of completion tokens is recorded.\",\n    \"The total cost of the operation is recorded.\",\n    \"If an error occurs, its status code is recorded.\",\n    \"Behavioral signals such as thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation are stored.\",\n    \"User requests to speak with a human or to provide an email address are stored as escalation signals.\",\n    \"The user may ask the same questions repeatedly.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"Thumbs\u2011up and thumbs\u2011down buttons are a very lightweight way for users to tell us whether the answer they just got was right or wrong.\",\n    \"When a user clicks thumbs up, we can treat that as a positive signal saying \u201cthis response was correct in this particular context.\u201d\",\n    \"When they click thumbs down, it\u2019s a negative signal saying \u201csomething went off\u2011track here.\u201d\",\n    \"We already capture a lot of surrounding information\u2014the original user question, the retrieved context the RAG system used, the exact output we generated, latency, token counts, any error codes, and even other behavioral cues.\",\n    \"The thumbs feedback becomes a label that we can attach to a fully documented interaction.\",\n    \"By collecting many of those labeled interactions over time, we can start to assemble a \u201cgolden\u201d dataset: a set of examples where we know, with high confidence, which responses are good and which are bad.\",\n    \"The positive (thumbs\u2011up) examples give us high\u2011quality, correct answer\u2013context pairs that we can use as reference or training material.\",\n    \"The negative (thumbs\u2011down) examples flag responses that need to be examined, corrected, or removed, helping us prune noisy or incorrect data.\",\n    \"The thumbs signals let us automatically annotate large volumes of chat logs with correctness information.\",\n    \"That annotation is exactly what we need to build a reliable, curated golden dataset for future evaluation or model improvement.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response fully addressed how thumbs up/down signals can build a golden dataset with no irrelevant content; it\u2019s already perfect, so it cannot be higher.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"The thumbs\u2011up and thumbs\u2011down buttons provide a lightweight way for users to indicate whether an answer is right or wrong.\",\n    \"When a user clicks thumbs up, it can be treated as a positive signal that the response was correct in that particular context.\",\n    \"When a user clicks thumbs down, it is a negative signal that something went off\u2011track.\",\n    \"We already capture surrounding information such as the original user question, retrieved context, the exact output, latency, token counts, error codes, and other behavioral cues.\",\n    \"The thumbs feedback becomes a label that can be attached to a fully documented interaction.\",\n    \"By collecting many labeled interactions over time, we can assemble a \u201cgolden\u201d dataset of examples with high confidence about which responses are good and which are bad.\",\n    \"Positive (thumbs\u2011up) examples provide high\u2011quality, correct answer\u2013context pairs for reference or training.\",\n    \"Negative (thumbs\u2011down) examples flag responses that need to be examined, corrected, or removed, helping prune noisy or incorrect data.\",\n    \"Thumbs signals allow automatic annotation of large volumes of chat logs with correctness information.\",\n    \"This annotation is needed to build a reliable, curated golden dataset for future evaluation or model improvement.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The statement describes other captured metadata rather than directly explaining how thumbs\\u2011up/down signals contribute to building a golden dataset.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"When we ask a language model to work through a math problem \\u201cstep by step,\\u201d we\\u2019re basically giving it a little roadmap that tells the model to pause after each tiny piece of reasoning instead of trying to jump straight to the final answer. The idea, which the course material calls *chain\\u2011of\\u2011thought* prompting, is that the model first writes out the intermediate calculations\\u2014like \\u201c48 divided by\\u202f2 is\\u202f24\\u201d and then \\u201c48\\u202f+\\u202f24\\u202f=\\u202f72\\u201d\\u2014and only after those pieces are on the page does it present the final result.\\n\\nWhy does that help? Think of it as the difference between a student who scribbles a whole solution in one rush and a student who writes each line of work on the board, checking the numbers as they go. By forcing the model to spell out each operation, we give it a chance to catch a slip before it propagates. The context even points out that when chain\\u2011of\\u2011thought is allowed, the chance of mistakes drops compared with a direct answer.\\n\\nThere\\u2019s also a practical side to the prompt. Because the model is now generating a longer piece of text (the whole reasoning chain), we usually keep the sampling temperature near zero. A low temperature makes the output more deterministic, so the model isn\\u2019t tempted to wander into creative but incorrect answers. Likewise, we set the maximum\\u2011token limit high enough that the model can finish the whole chain; if the limit is too low, the reasoning might be cut off before it reaches the answer.\\n\\nIn short, a step\\u2011by\\u2011step prompt guides the LLM to break the problem into smaller, verifiable parts, reduces the likelihood of a stray error, and works best when the decoding settings (low temperature, sufficient token budget) keep the generation focused and complete.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"how does a step-by-step prompt help an llm solve a math problem?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"kind of system prompt that see this question and try to solve it step by step like here the answer is also shown Natalia sold 48 divided by 2 means 24 clips in may Natalia sold 48 plus 24 clips all together this is getting solved step by step and is reaching the answer if you are not allowing chain of thought and you are telling to directly answer this then chances of mistakes are more so in GSM 8k also chain of thought is allowed again, here we are just talking about the options available while constructing the prompt few shot, zero shot, chain of thought or normal after that you see things in your decoding and sampling configuration you keep temperature value around zero because if you increase it more than zero then creative answers start coming and every time they start varying so temperature is mostly around zero for such benchmarks then you set a max token if you set a very less max token then it can be since chain of thought is triggered and while thinking in the reach the\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because there are no contradictions, indicating perfect faithfulness.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Truths (limit=None):\n[\n    \"Natalia sold 48 clips, and dividing by 2 gives 24 clips.\",\n    \"Adding 48 clips and 24 clips results in a total of 72 clips.\",\n    \"In the GSM8K benchmark, chain-of-thought prompting is allowed.\",\n    \"Prompting options include few-shot, zero-shot, chain-of-thought, and standard prompting.\",\n    \"Decoding and sampling configurations often set the temperature value near zero.\",\n    \"Increasing the temperature above zero can lead to more creative and varied answers.\",\n    \"For certain benchmarks, temperature is typically kept around zero.\",\n    \"Setting a very low max token limit may affect the generation of chain-of-thought responses.\"\n] \n \nClaims:\n[\n    \"When we ask a language model to work through a math problem \u201cstep by step,\u201d we\u2019re basically giving it a little roadmap that tells the model to pause after each tiny piece of reasoning instead of trying to jump straight to the final answer.\",\n    \"The idea, which the course material calls *chain\u2011of\u2011thought* prompting, is that the model first writes out the intermediate calculations\u2014like \u201c48 divided by\u202f2 is\u202f24\u201d and then \u201c48\u202f+\u202f24\u202f=\u202f72\u201d\u2014and only after those pieces are on the page does it present the final result.\",\n    \"Why does that help? Think of it as the difference between a student who scribbles a whole solution in one rush and a student who writes each line of work on the board, checking the numbers as they go.\",\n    \"By forcing the model to spell out each operation, we give it a chance to catch a slip before it propagates.\",\n    \"The context even points out that when chain\u2011of\u2011thought is allowed, the chance of mistakes drops compared with a direct answer.\",\n    \"There\u2019s also a practical side to the prompt.\",\n    \"Because the model is now generating a longer piece of text (the whole reasoning chain), we usually keep the sampling temperature near zero.\",\n    \"A low temperature makes the output more deterministic, so the model isn\u2019t tempted to wander into creative but incorrect answers.\",\n    \"Likewise, we set the maximum\u2011token limit high enough that the model can finish the whole chain; if the limit is too low, the reasoning might be cut off before it reaches the answer.\",\n    \"In short, a step\u2011by\u2011step prompt guides the LLM to break the problem into smaller, verifiable parts, reduces the likelihood of a stray error, and works best when the decoding settings (low temperature, sufficient token budget) keep the generation focused and complete.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not mention giving the model a roadmap or pausing after each reasoning step, so this claim is not supported by the provided facts.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context contains no analogy comparing the model's behavior to a student writing on a board, so this claim is not backed by the given information.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The retrieval context does not state that spelling out each operation helps catch errors before they propagate.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"While chain\\u2011of\\u2011thought prompting is allowed, the context does not mention a reduction in mistake probability compared to direct answers.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The context does not discuss a practical side to the prompt, so this claim is not supported.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response fully addressed the question with no irrelevant statements, achieving perfect relevance.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"When we ask a language model to work through a math problem \u201cstep by step,\u201d we\u2019re basically giving it a little roadmap that tells the model to pause after each tiny piece of reasoning instead of trying to jump straight to the final answer.\",\n    \"The idea, which the course material calls chain\u2011of\u2011thought prompting, is that the model first writes out the intermediate calculations\u2014like \u201c48 divided by\u202f2 is\u202f24\u201d and then \u201c48\u202f+\u202f24\u202f=\u202f72\u201d\u2014and only after those pieces are on the page does it present the final result.\",\n    \"Think of it as the difference between a student who scribbles a whole solution in one rush and a student who writes each line of work on the board, checking the numbers as they go.\",\n    \"By forcing the model to spell out each operation, we give it a chance to catch a slip before it propagates.\",\n    \"The context even points out that when chain\u2011of\u2011thought is allowed, the chance of mistakes drops compared with a direct answer.\",\n    \"There\u2019s also a practical side to the prompt.\",\n    \"Because the model is now generating a longer piece of text (the whole reasoning chain), we usually keep the sampling temperature near zero.\",\n    \"A low temperature makes the output more deterministic, so the model isn\u2019t tempted to wander into creative but incorrect answers.\",\n    \"Likewise, we set the maximum\u2011token limit high enough that the model can finish the whole chain; if the limit is too low, the reasoning might be cut off before it reaches the answer.\",\n    \"In short, a step\u2011by\u2011step prompt guides the LLM to break the problem into smaller, verifiable parts, reduces the likelihood of a stray error, and works best when the decoding settings (low temperature, sufficient token budget) keep the generation focused and complete.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"idk\",\n        \"reason\": \"The statement is a vague introductory comment that does not directly explain how a step\\u2011by\\u2011step prompt helps solve a math problem, though it could be seen as peripheral context.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Sure, let\\u2019s break down what \\u201ccomputed\\u201d and \\u201ccaptured\\u201d signals mean when you\\u2019re doing an online evaluation of a chatbot.\\n\\nFirst, think of a signal as any piece of information that tells you something about how the system is behaving or how the user is reacting. In an online setting you can get two kinds of signals.\\n\\nA **captured signal** is something that\\u2019s already there for you to record; you don\\u2019t have to do any extra work to derive it. It\\u2019s basically a raw observation that you can store directly. For example, if a user clicks a thumbs\\u2011up or a thumbs\\u2011down button during the conversation, that click itself is a captured signal\\u2014you just log it in your tracking system (like LangSmith) exactly as it appears. The same goes for latency (the amount of time the chatbot took to produce an answer), the number of tokens used, or the monetary cost of a conversation. All of these are already measured by the system, so you simply capture and store them without any further calculation.\\n\\nA **computed signal**, on the other hand, is something you have to derive yourself. These aren\\u2019t given to you automatically; you need to calculate or infer them from other data. The context doesn\\u2019t give a concrete example, but the idea is that you would take existing information\\u2014perhaps the raw logs, timestamps, or user actions\\u2014and apply some logic or formula to produce a new metric. Because you have to \\u201cfigure out\\u201d or compute it, it\\u2019s called a computed signal.\\n\\nSo, in short: captured signals are ready\\u2011made observations like thumbs\\u2011up/down, latency, token count, or cost that you just store, while computed signals are metrics you have to calculate yourself from the data you collect.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what are computed and captured signals in online evaluation?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"not? Okay? So there are actually two types of signals which you have to focus on. The first one is computed and the second one is captured signals. Okay? So computed signals are those that you have to calculate or figure out. And captured signals are those that are already present and you have to store them somewhere. I will give you both the examples. For example, captured signal is thumbs up, thumbs down. If the user did thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then this is a signal that the user is giving me. So I am directly storing it in the langsmith. Now I didn't need to calculate anything here. The user said thumbs down, I have to store it as it is. So we call this captured signal. One more example is latency. To do any particular answer, how much time did my chatbot take? Whatever time it took, I have to store it in the langsmith. Now I don't need to calculate anything here. Cost per conversation. How much token did I spend? How much money did I spend? Again, it is\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{