omputed signal is something you have to derive yourself.\",\n    \"These aren\u2019t given to you automatically; you need to calculate or infer them from other data.\",\n    \"You would take existing information\u2014perhaps the raw logs, timestamps, or user actions\u2014and apply some logic or formula to produce a new metric.\",\n    \"Because you have to \u201cfigure out\u201d or compute it, it\u2019s called a computed signal.\",\n    \"Captured signals are ready\u2011made observations like thumbs\u2011up/down, latency, token count, or cost that you just store.\",\n    \"Computed signals are metrics you have to calculate yourself from the data you collect.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the response directly answered the question about computed and captured signals in online evaluation with no irrelevant material.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Statements:\n[\n    \"Sure, let\u2019s break down what \u201ccomputed\u201d and \u201ccaptured\u201d signals mean when you\u2019re doing an online evaluation of a chatbot.\",\n    \"Think of a signal as any piece of information that tells you something about how the system is behaving or how the user is reacting.\",\n    \"In an online setting you can get two kinds of signals.\",\n    \"A captured signal is something that\u2019s already there for you to record; you don\u2019t have to do any extra work to derive it.\",\n    \"It\u2019s basically a raw observation that you can store directly.\",\n    \"If a user clicks a thumbs\u2011up or a thumbs\u2011down button during the conversation, that click itself is a captured signal.\",\n    \"You just log it in your tracking system (like LangSmith) exactly as it appears.\",\n    \"Latency (the amount of time the chatbot took to produce an answer) is a captured signal.\",\n    \"The number of tokens used is a captured signal.\",\n    \"The monetary cost of a conversation is a captured signal.\",\n    \"All of these are already measured by the system, so you simply capture and store them without any further calculation.\",\n    \"A computed signal is something you have to derive yourself.\",\n    \"These aren\u2019t given to you automatically; you need to calculate or infer them from other data.\",\n    \"The context doesn\u2019t give a concrete example.\",\n    \"You would take existing information\u2014perhaps the raw logs, timestamps, or user actions\u2014and apply some logic or formula to produce a new metric.\",\n    \"Because you have to \u201cfigure out\u201d or compute it, it\u2019s called a computed signal.\",\n    \"Captured signals are ready\u2011made observations like thumbs\u2011up/down, latency, token count, or cost that you just store.\",\n    \"Computed signals are metrics you have to calculate yourself from the data you collect.\"\n] \n \nVerdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": null\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"In online evaluation we rely on two kinds of information to tell us whether the chatbot is behaving as we expect:\\u202fcaptured signals and\\u202fcomputed signals.\\n\\nA captured signal is something that the system already records as the user interacts with the bot. You don\\u2019t have to do any extra processing\\u2014just store the value that\\u2019s produced. The classic examples are the thumbs\\u2011up or thumbs\\u2011down feedback a user clicks after a response, the latency of a reply, or the cost incurred for handling a request. Because these data points are emitted directly by the user interface or the runtime environment, we simply capture them and log them (for instance, in a tool like LangSmith) without any further calculation.\\n\\nA computed signal, on the other hand, is a metric that isn\\u2019t emitted automatically; you have to derive it from the conversation data. To obtain it you set up an evaluator that processes the chat logs and produces a score. A typical example is a \\u201cfaithfulness\\u201d or \\u201challucination\\u201d metric that measures how accurately the bot\\u2019s answer reflects the source information. Since the raw chat alone doesn\\u2019t contain that score, you send the exchange to an online evaluator, which runs the necessary algorithm and returns the computed value. This requires building a pipeline that calculates the metric each time a new interaction occurs.\\n\\nSo, captured signals are directly observed and stored (like thumbs up/down, latency, cost), while computed signals are derived by running an additional evaluation step (such as faithfulness or hallucination scores) on the logged conversation. Both types together give you a complete picture of how the deployed chatbot is performing.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what are computed and captured signals in online evaluation?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\". Now comes step 2. Now before discussing step 2, we have to find out what matters in online evaluation. What kind of signals do you have to pay attention to? What things will you understand that whether your chatbot is working properly or not? Okay? So there are actually two types of signals which you have to focus on. The first one is computed and the second one is captured signals. Okay? So computed signals are those that you have to calculate or figure out. And captured signals are those that are already present and you have to store them somewhere. I will give you both the examples. For example, captured signal is thumbs up, thumbs down. If the user did thumbs up or thumbs down during the conversation, then this is a signal that the user is giving me\", \". So these are the examples or signals that you only capture and store in the langsmith. You don't need to calculate anything in this. Now let's talk about the computed one. The computed one is the signal that you have to calculate. You have to figure out. For example, you want to see in the online production setup how much is the faithfulness of your chatbot. Now this is something that you won't get only by storing the chat. You will have to make an evaluator for this. An online evaluator. You will send that chat to this online evaluator. This online evaluator will calculate and compute how much is the faithfulness score. So this is an example of a quantity or metric or signal that we have to calculate\", \". The simplest definition of online eval is, That it is a different type of evaluation, Which we run on production, After our software is deployed. And the biggest characteristic of it is, That it works without an answer. It works without a golden data set. This is the biggest feature of online eval. And that is why it is super critical, Because it helps us, That our software, which is deployed, Keeps running properly. It tells us that, There is no problem with online. In that sense, online evals are super important. So, on the basis of the discussion till now, If I quickly lay out a difference, Between offline and online, There are 4-5 pointers. First of all, offline eval, Is before deployment, After online eval deployment\", \". What are the general metrics and signals tracked via online evaluation? Look, again, this is also a question that we will discuss now. Look, just the one that is written on the screen in front of you. Now I am going to discuss that what kind of signals you work on online, what you see, where can there be a problem. We will discuss that now. So don't worry, we are going to discuss all this. If you have any other question, ask. Okay. Shruti Singh, I didn't get the distribution part. Shruti, it was very simple\", \". Yes, sure. Computed and captured logins work together. As I said, you make multiple pipelines. So we are discussing captured eval pipelines. This is not exactly a pipeline, to be honest. There is no pipeline here. There are a series of steps. You will see a pipeline in the computed one because you will have to set up an evaluator there. So you will understand from that. Is there any other question at this point? Okay. Okay. So now let's discuss the main part, the most interesting part. When you have to track a computed quantity in production. Let's take an example. Let's say there is an example of a pipeline that is not in production. Let's say the example is Let me think. Let's say the example is hallucination. Okay\", \"ation does not stop I had taught you this and we are going to do the same thing here once the software is deployed even after that we will keep evaluating it and this evaluation is called online evaluation now what all things you will do here I will tell you quickly here you measure 3-4 things once the software is online you measure 3-4 things first you capture some things like every time when a student comes to our chatbot and asks doubts then to answer his question what is the latency how much is the cost did he give thumbs up or thumbs down you capture all these signals quickly write to me in the chat to do this whole thing which software can we use give me one software name which we can use, I have shown you in the past which software can we use for this work to collect these signals L\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Sure, let\\u2019s break down what \\u201cbenchmark saturation\\u201d means in plain language.  \\n\\nWhen researchers first introduce a benchmark\\u2014say a set of math problems or a question\\u2011answering test\\u2014no model has seen it before, so the scores are usually low and there\\u2019s a lot of room to tell the models apart. As the community builds more capable language models, those models get better and better at the same benchmark. Eventually, the scores of many different systems start clustering at the top end\\u2014often in the mid\\u201190\\u202fpercent range for things like GSM\\u20118K or MMLU. At that point the benchmark can no longer serve its original purpose, which is to discriminate between stronger and weaker models. Because every model is getting essentially the same high mark, the test has become \\u201csaturated.\\u201d  \\n\\nWhen saturation happens, the benchmark is typically retired: it\\u2019s taken out of active use because it no longer provides useful signal about relative model quality. Then a new benchmark is introduced, and the cycle repeats\\u2014new benchmark, low initial scores, gradual improvement, and finally saturation again.  \\n\\nSo, in short, a benchmark is said to be saturated when it has been solved so well by most current models that it can\\u2019t meaningfully differentiate their capabilities any more, prompting the community to move on to a fresh, more challenging test.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what does it mean for a benchmark to get saturated?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"me place this is called benchmark saturation this means that this benchmark is no longer useful you can't differentiate the models because if all have the same marks then it will be called exam then how will you know who is good and who is bad in that case the benchmark is saturated it is removed and then a new benchmark is brought and then the industry starts working on that so this example of gsm 8k this is a saturated benchmark it is not used now everyone gets 94-97% I said your MMLU that is a saturated benchmark sui bench is also saturated so mostly there is a life cycle of benchmarks all the models are difficult then gradually the models improve everyone starts scoring well then your benchmark is saturated then it is retired then replaced with a new benchmark that is how the benchmark\", \"nchmark is saturated then it is retired then replaced with a new benchmark that is how the benchmarking process is done after that this is also a problem of configuration gaming configuration gaming is basically a term where frontier ai labs when they benchmark then they create a configuration related mess means they will give their model the most favorable conditions and the rival model the default conditions or you can think what was the point I forgot but roughly you can think that the basic idea of configuration gaming is that the configuration run you are given you provide the most favorable conditions for example you are benchmarking gsm 8k and there you gave your model the tool of python interpreter so it solved all the mathematical questions so obviously the score will be better so\", \"public benchmarks so the solution is to use private benchmarks there are many private benchmarks we will discuss in the next class or use dynamic benchmarks these are like static benchmarks once the data set published in research is the same data set since last 5 years but there are certain dynamic benchmarks as well where on a daily basis or in a particular window the data set keeps updating so those types of data sets can be used but benchmark contamination is a proper thing second there is one more thing benchmark saturation benchmark saturation is a process where a benchmark comes in the picture let's say in 2021 so now this is new and no model has any idea about this benchmark no one has seen it so on these questions most of the times your models poorly perform 25% score or 36% score\", \"t incorrect in the sense either the answer is not given properly or the answer is not being said properly so these researchers found this finding so this is how we found out that no one can score 100% on MM LU people were thinking that we are reaching 90-92 but no one is able to go on it so we found out the reason when MM LU Redux paper came so after this we understood that this benchmark is saturated and now we have to do something new that's when MM LU Pro paper came and they made all these changes which I have told you above so if you talk about the current status of MM LU Pro it came in 2024, now it is running in 2026 and now it is also nearing saturation so people are now scoring around 80-90 if we talk about its task and data set details so here there are 12000 questions 14000 questi\", \"which are nearing saturation which are saturated and which are deprecated and you can filter out from here as well and everything is explained in detail I have shown this in the last class as well but now we have deployed it as well and added more benchmarks and in the next 2 weeks we will add more additional so if you don't want to spend your time watching long lectures about these benchmarks what you can do is you can simply go to this website and you can read about whatever benchmarks you would like to learn about so this is the solution, I hope you are understanding and you are understanding my reasoning as well why I can't teach you everything because it will take a lot of time so this is the whole discussion, is it clear? you can bookmark this url benchwiki.versal.app you will get t\", \"years ago because the capability of the LLM as a judge has increased in the last two years I hope I understood what this means so this is the benchmark basically got saturated because its data started getting used in the alignment stage due to that all LLM's started performing well over time due to which it got saturated this is the story of this particular but very important benchmark because this is the first time we were told that having more capability in a model does not necessarily mean that the model will be more aligned and then people started working consciously so this is also a very important benchmark in that sense, now it is not used yeah let me know if you have any doubts this was the second one any questions? Sir what do we exactly do? we just do the same thing we just do th\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"th